    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_ZD63T58V6YJR8Y3TJJ090QAZVF",
      id: "parse-node-source-extent",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/compiler/parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "Both parsers record every node's full source extent when they build it",
        boundary: "PRODUCER CONTRACT: every successfully published source occurrence has its complete source interval retained from the parse that establishes it -- including suffixes consumed after its identity was minted (v1 maybe_optional reuses a type's identity after consuming `?`) and excluding separators outside it (parse_import consumes trailing newlines its extent must not cover) -- and the parser publishes those intervals in its final source artifact, following the accepted branch through speculative parsing. The interval is recorded at SYNTAX COMPLETION, not at identity minting: minting and completion diverge (optional-type suffix, interpolation fragments minted together after the string ends, a property and its value minted together over different regions, binary expressions whose diagnostic span is the operator). The population is every source occurrence: terminals, production wrappers, infix applications, sequence/repeat composites that are not synthetic, nested productions and the root; an empty match records no interval and synthetic scaffolding records none. SOURCE SCOPES THAT SEMANTIC SIMPLIFICATION ERASES ARE RETAINED with their ordered containment (v1 parse_paren_expr returns the inner expression; parse_block returns a sole statement), in the parser's source side table, without adding semantic nodes. v2: SpanIndex extents beside DerivedBy lineage. v1: the same side-table shape published with the parse result, not a Node field. Diagnostic focus, source extent and causal lineage stay three facts (gunbc.design_argument::interface_realization_policy). The existing v1 import-extent reader (the consumed-window rescan) reads the recorded interval instead.",
        displaced_cost: "Consumers that need containment (annotation attachment, import editing) cannot tell whether a position is inside a node or after its closing delimiter, so each reconstructs a range from descendants or tokens -- an approximate second answer to a fact the parser had and discarded (gunbc.design_argument::minimize_redundancy, gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary).",
        first_slice: "v2: production wrappers record their interval at completion (block reaches its closing brace); then infix, repeat and root paths; then v1 through the same contract.",
        red_control: "Discriminating cases, the shared contract that both parsers read (only the v1 half is required by this row; the v2 half is its sibling row v2-parse-node-source-extent-witness-is-executable): a position between a block's last statement and its closing brace is inside the block and one after the brace is outside (an interval ending at the last child is RED); `((x))` retains both paren scopes around one occurrence; a singleton block retains its scope; `Int?` covers the `?`; an import's interval stops before its trailing newlines; an empty production and an abandoned speculative branch record nothing.",
        out_of_scope: "Comment attachment itself (parse-comment-gap-attachment). Changing what span means in v1. The v2 half, which is its own row (v2-parse-node-source-extent-witness-is-executable).",
        handback: "THE V1 HALF COMPLETE: every red control above green on v1 under the probe pair, and the import rescan deleted. The v2 half is deferred to its sibling row, v2-parse-node-source-extent-witness-is-executable, and is not required here."
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11 from the in-body comment design: the producer boundary is the parser's published result (gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary), so the repair is here, not in the annotation binder."), on: "2026-11-23", note: "Split (vivid-swift-693, neat-badger-614): the v1 half keeps a probe-form red control, given below the discriminating cases; the v2 half is its own defect row, v2-parse-node-source-extent-witness-is-executable, because its witness is never executable on any route yet. This row's red_control text is otherwise unchanged. V1 PROBE: red_at 007830dc294bc7013e9942ce6a9cb9f38fa3a624 on red-control/parse-node-source-extent, whose first parent is red_base 6d2115d1e6e700671f0ee8317ae6a2e1dfa66da6; green_at 6d2115d1e6e700671f0ee8317ae6a2e1dfa66da6. The plant touches src/v1/02_parse.dag and src/v2/std/provenance.dag (2 files, 3 insertions, 3 deletions) so that no parser records source extents; the v1 witness is src/v1/tests/claim/parse_source_extent_control_test.dag.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-compiler-live", contribution: "The live compiler's parse result must carry the source facts its consumers need instead of leaving each to re-derive them."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_GSPGVKP2MWQET36DMTTXKT0H6Q",
      id: "match-exhaustiveness-imported-projection-lookup",
      owner: "compiler-guarantee",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/test/claim/match_exhaustiveness_coproduct_witness.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "Exhaustiveness resolves a field projection of an imported authority's type, not only the current module's local types",
        boundary: "A field projection of an imported type (RequiredFloorDisposition) retains the nominal imported type name, but the exhaustiveness lookup asks only the current module's LOCAL type environment for its declaration, so a match over the projection is neither checked nor reported. This is a gap in the shipped match_exhaustiveness_coproduct_witness fix itself; it is not floor-exhaustiveness-refusal, whose head-built binary shows the identical failure set. The producing symbol is not yet named; the row is not dispatchable until the owning lane names it. No behaviour is claimed beyond the two names below.",
        displaced_cost: "A match over an imported authority's projected field passes unchecked or reports a wrong count, so a missing arm is invisible exactly where the authority is imported rather than local (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed).",
        first_slice: "Resolve the projected type through the imported authority's declaration, then re-run the witness module.",
        red_control: "Two witnesses of dag/test/claim/match_exhaustiveness_coproduct_witness.dag are RED on main with the released claim_batch whose sha256 is 9cb21df4c20325fc53ba4187cf4c55bd08037862a45d3900c5cc5519e360a177 (a binary hash, not a commit): w_imported_projected_missing_arm_reports_one_non_exhaustive and w_imported_projected_exhaustive_match_observes_zero_non_exhaustive; 24 of 26 pass. The same 24/2 failure set was observed with the head-built binary of floor-exhaustiveness-refusal (bold-pike-529). Provenance (bold-pike-529, from bold-ibex-678's run): the witness inputs were evaluated at source-tree commit 6d2115d1e6e700671f0ee8317ae6a2e1dfa66da6, and the released binary was built from commit f77c9d2341c2a26620b42d2086f7c5c5750ed62e (release-bins preimage, CI run 45059586538; its source_closure is a TREE sha, c849aef90b7498c9de2fa3121f7de19f13495894, not a commit). The binary was not built at the observed tree, so the red_at-equals-build-tree rule is not met and red_at and green_at stay OPEN: the fix builds claim_batch at the commit it pins and observes the pair there. The control is a binary built at red_at over this witness module, per the red_at equals build-tree rule.",
        out_of_scope: "floor-exhaustiveness-refusal. Any other coproduct-exhaustiveness behaviour.",
        handback: "Return the fix, both named witnesses PASS with the claim_batch sha256 and the commit it was built from, and the 24 passing names still passing."
      )),
      parent: in_project(project: "compiler-guarantee", contribution: "Closed variants must eliminate exhaustively on the ordinary compiler floor, including through imported authorities."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_WTYZMZ8DGPYGZF730BASZ18RAH",
      id: "v2-parse-node-source-extent-witness-is-executable",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/test/claim/provenance/parse_stamp_span_index.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The v2 half of parse-node-source-extent has a witness that runs",
        boundary: "The v2 witness for the source-extent contract of parse-node-source-extent, src/v2/test/claim/provenance/parse_stamp_span_index.dag, has no top-level import block at main (8,963 bytes, zero imports), so it has never been executable on any route, so no v2 reading of that contract exists. gunbc#16911 repairs parse_production_extent.dag only and does not touch this file; this row owns the v2 half only, and its own worker adds the import block to the witness file and then executes it and the v1 half stays on parse-node-source-extent. The contract itself is not restated here: it is the producer contract of that row.",
        displaced_cost: "A witness that never executes reads as coverage of the v2 parser while checking nothing, so a v2 extent defect is invisible and the v1 half's probe says nothing about it (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed, done means executed).",
        first_slice: "Add the import block to src/v2/test/claim/provenance/parse_stamp_span_index.dag, execute it on a route, then run it against the v2 parser unchanged. It does not wait on gunbc#16911.",
        red_control: "The file failing to resolve or load at main, with that output executed and pasted; green is its witnesses evaluating, with real PASS/FAIL lines. NONE OBSERVED YET: red_at and green_at are OPEN and no sha is claimed; the pair is written as data only once the witness executes (red_at, green_at, and a probe's red_base and probe_ref).",
        out_of_scope: "The v1 half (parse-node-source-extent). Changing the source-extent contract.",
        handback: "Return the merged import block, the witness executing on a named route, and its red and green shas."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-compiler-live", contribution: "The live compiler's parse result must carry the source facts its consumers need instead of leaving each to re-derive them."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_ZGAW39FNPPF9T1TMMXJSDD2CWW",
      id: "claim-batch-false-verdict-carries-a-located-reason",
      owner: "compiler-guarantee",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v1/stage0/src/bin/claim_batch.rs"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "A claim that answers Bool(false) can say why under claim_batch",
        boundary: "claim_batch prints only PASS and FAIL with the claim name for a test fn that returns Bool(false). The arms that print a reason are the ClaimOutcome::ExitFailure arms, and those change the verdict class (code 2 renders as unobserved), so a witness cannot both answer false and say why. The companion convention, a <x>_passes fn with a <x>_failure_receipt fn, fires only through seed_runner_bool_false_failure_detail, which is reached only when a witness calls its builtin itself on the seed runner path; claim_batch never calls it for a test fn returning Bool(false). A red wall therefore prints a bare FAIL line (bold-lynx-262: retained_rust_kernel_wall_holds_against_live_tree, at main and at his head), and the offender is known only from a count derived outside the claim. Reported by warm-fox-517, who checked it against the source. The seed is semantics-frozen (gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing::v1_seed_standing): the repair is admitted only if it serves the v2 self-host program, and that is a question for the owning lane, not settled here.",
        displaced_cost: "Every control of the shape wall-returns-false is red with no reason, so each reader re-derives the offender by hand outside the claim (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed, a refusal is loud, typed and located).",
        first_slice: "On the native runner, project the companion convention's detail into the FAIL line of a Bool(false) verdict, leaving verdict class and exit code unchanged.",
        red_control: "NONE OBSERVED YET: red_at and green_at are OPEN and no sha is claimed. The control is two witnesses: one plants a single offender and shows its name in the FAIL line, and one shows the verdict class and the exit code unchanged for the same claim. Each is executed on a named route with its PASS/FAIL lines pasted.",
        out_of_scope: "Changing any verdict class or exit code. Which route (seed claim_batch or native runner) carries it; the owning lane decides.",
        handback: "Return the change, both witnesses with PASS/FAIL lines and the commit they were built from, and the bare FAIL line of the wall above now carrying its offender."
      )),
      parent: in_project(project: "compiler-guarantee", contribution: "A refusal is loud, typed and located, including a red wall reported through the claim runner."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_MX72H51BBE6ZH57APJDRG7VA22",
      id: "serve-entry-compile-inhabitance-wet-lane-cost",
      owner: "self-host",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/test/claim/live_deploy/pre_swap_admission_serve_compile_inhabitance_witness.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The serve-entry compile inhabitance claim stops paying a 31-minute whole-closure compile",
        boundary: "observe_serve_entry_compile_clean (gunbc.live_deploy.pre_swap_admission) has two callers, both claims. pre_swap_admission_serve_compile_inhabitance_witness imports and calls it, and its own real_execution_home_doc says the checkout's built gunbc compiles a serve entry to the dag target, so it is a LocalRepoWetLane claim; the other caller, pre_swap_admission_witness, is a fixture with repo_root and bin_path set to /nonexistent and costs nothing. The deploy step no longer runs this compile (it runs the served command), so the cost is the wet lane's, not a deploy's. Measured baseline (gentle-tern-663, 957fa04f, gunbc 83c2770d): 1,871.79 s wall, 1,858.81 s user, 14,794,308 kB peak RSS, exit 0, closure about 3,666 files, of which 1,798,764 ms sits unattributed inside one compile_transaction. The claim pays that to prove a verdict fold reads an exit code and some error lines. If the wet-lane claim is the wrong shape and should not compile the whole closure at all, the owning lane decides between making the compile cheaper and making the claim smaller.",
        displaced_cost: "The wet lane's wall clock and the CI budget that absorbs it: about 31 minutes and 14.8 GB for one inhabitance claim (gunbc.design_argument::how_to_work, a proven cost-shape defect is always fixed).",
        first_slice: "Attribute the 1,798,764 ms unattributed inside compile_transaction before anyone optimises it.",
        red_control: "NONE OBSERVED YET: no before and after pair exists and no sha is claimed. The control is the same claim at a named revision with a receipt under its stated budget, against the baseline above, with the verdict fold still discriminating a refused compile from a clean one.",
        out_of_scope: "Changing what the deploy step runs. Persisting the program across restarts (demand-serve-program-image).",
        handback: "The attribution of the unattributed time, the change (cheaper compile or smaller claim, with the reason), the measured wall and RSS before and after at the same revision, and the claim still red on a refused compile."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "demand-engine", contribution: "A deploy pays for the serve entry's compile only what the change requires."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_JXW6HYEDDAHGZ77HCGTYSF3FX3",
      id: "parse-comment-gap-attachment",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/std/source_annotation.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "A comment attaches to the gap between its neighbouring nodes, anywhere in a file",
        boundary: "std.source_annotation replaces the single-subject anchor with AnnotationScope = FileScope | SourceScope and AnnotationGap { scope, before?, after? }. The scope is the innermost retained source scope containing the comment (the file when none does); before/after are its ORDERED DIRECT SOURCE-SYNTAX CHILDREN, with parser scaffolding (v2 production markers, sequence/repeat spines, projection edges) projected transparently. For a call `foo(a, b)` with a comment on its own line between the arguments, the scope is the argument-list scope with before = a and after = b, never a descendant of an argument. File scope covers leading and trailing trivia: the lexer's final source position is retained in its artifact rather than dropped, so a banner above the module header and a comment after the last item both land. One rule replaces the positional cases: BodyGrainNotModeled, TrailingNotModeled, UnattachedAtScopeEnd, module_header_gap_subject and v1 module_item_extent_ends are deleted. v1 v1.compiler.annotation_bind and the v2 authored parse both feed the one attach_annotations. Anchors reference existing occurrences and mint none (gunbc.design_argument::source_annotations). Which node a comment documents is derived from the gap, never stored.",
        displaced_cost: "A comment inside a function body is a hard compile error today, and each further position (trailing, before a closing brace, file top) is its own special case or refusal.",
        first_slice: "The shared fold over a supplied roster: in-body, before-closing-brace, trailing and file-top comments each land their expected gap.",
        red_control: "A comment inside f's body never attaches to the next declaration g; the argument-list case lands before = a, after = b. Adding, removing or moving a comment leaves semantic hash, occurrence ids and emitted bytes unchanged. Round trip: a negative control where allocation ordinals stay equal but the target syntax changes must DISAGREE.",
        out_of_scope: "Computing extents (parse-node-source-extent). Doc-comment semantics beyond the derived documents-view.",
        handback: "In-body comments compile in the corpus and the three refusal arms are deleted. The round trip, over a named route (the v1 render/reparse path, not a full formatter), agrees on a STRUCTURAL cross-parse key for scope and both neighbours -- never occurrence ordinals, which are equal across parses by position only (std.source_annotation KeyedAnnotationRow) -- and preserves normalized text, source order and multiplicity, including duplicate comments in one gap."
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11. Depends on parse-node-source-extent: attachment reads the parser's extents rather than reconstructing them.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-compiler-live", contribution: "Authors can annotate any position in a .dag file without a compile error, under one attachment rule."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_J2HQNCTQJGT2X0MTR31TB6HE8F",
      id: "parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/compiler/parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The v2 parser attaches comments by the same gap rule as v1",
        boundary: "v2 projects a std.source_annotation AnnotationRegion roster from its parse: each composite's extent from v2.std.provenance span_index_extent (an Unrepresentable extent contributes no region and is reported, never read as empty) and its parent from the parse tree, with parser scaffolding -- production markers, sequence and repeat spines, projection edges -- projected transparently so a region's children are its ORDERED DIRECT SOURCE-SYNTAX CHILDREN. The v2 capture row gains the preceded_by_blank_line and preceded_by_annotation_line bits the walk can observe from the line-prefix state it already threads. The lexer's final source position is NOT needed: under the gap rule file scope is the residual scope, chosen when no region contains a capture, so it carries no extent. parse_module gains an authored sibling that calls attach_annotations, the same fold v1 calls, producing AuthoredParseArtifact.",
        displaced_cost: "v2 drops every comment at the lexer, so a v2-built tool cannot see, keep or write back authored prose, and the AuthoredParseArtifact carrier has no producer.",
        first_slice: "A roster projected from one parsed module, with the argument-list control: a comment between two arguments lands before = a, after = b in the call's scope.",
        red_control: "A comment inside f's body never attaches to the next declaration g; a comment between call arguments lands between the argument productions, not on spine nodes; v1 and v2 name the same neighbour SOURCE TEXT for every comment between siblings both trees retain (structural keys are not comparable across the two trees: v1 keys name authored identifiers, v2 keys name productions), with the places the trees differ asserted rather than dropped.",
        out_of_scope: "The attachment rule and the v1 realization (parse-comment-gap-attachment).",
        handback: "parse_module_authored attaches every capture of a parsed module through attach_annotations, the roster disposition note on AuthoredParseArtifact is deleted, and the v1/v2 neighbour-text agreement control is green; its production consumer is parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2-consumed and its gate admission is parse-comment-gap-agreement-control-gated."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-compiler-live", contribution: "The v2 compiler keeps authored prose under the same gap rule as the seed."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_K7QW3M9TZP4XH2D6VB8NRC5JYE",
      id: "parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2-consumed",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/compiler/parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The authored v2 parse has a production consumer and an executed agreement control",
        boundary: "parse_module_authored attaches comments by the shared gap rule but nothing in the production parse path calls it, and the v1/v2 neighbour-text agreement control (v2.test.claim.manual.annotation_gap_v1_v2_agreement) sits under manual/, so no gated row executes it. A v2-built consumer of AuthoredParseArtifact lands and is executed by a claim naming it. Gate admission of the agreement control is its own row (parse-comment-gap-agreement-control-gated): the required-floor rosters have no executor on main today, so admitting the control onto one would green nothing.",
        displaced_cost: "Until consumed, the authored v2 parse is executed only by claims, and a v1/v2 divergence in attachment goes unseen by CI.",
        first_slice: "One production caller of parse_module_authored, exercised by an executed claim.",
        red_control: "Breaking the v2 roster (dropping infix applications as regions) turns the v1/v2 neighbour-text agreement control (v2.test.claim.manual.annotation_gap_v1_v2_agreement.v1_and_v2_agree_on_neighbour_text_holds) red, and restoring the roster greens it again.",
        out_of_scope: "The roster and the gap rule (parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2), and gate admission of the agreement control (parse-comment-gap-agreement-control-gated).",
        handback: "A production route calls parse_module_authored -- fn front_end_parse_step in v2.compiler.staged_front_end -- and its consumer claim staged_front_end_produces_the_authored_parse_for_an_annotated_module passes by name."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-compiler-live", contribution: "The v2 compiler keeps authored prose under the same gap rule as the seed."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_30G4GZEQCTUA45T46NVD37H29Q",
      id: "parse-comment-gap-agreement-control-gated",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: concat(carrier(path: "src/v2/test/claim/manual/annotation_gap_v1_v2_agreement.dag"), carrier(path: "src/v2/workflow/required_floor.dag")),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The v1/v2 agreement control runs under a row the required gate judges",
        boundary: "The v1/v2 neighbour-text agreement control exists on main and passes, and nothing on a merge path decides its rung. Measured here: required_gate_admits (src/v2/workflow/required_floor.dag:900) and required_gate_authored_modules (:763) are real, and the host route that computes admission exists as v1_compiler.cli_run run_required_floor (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/required_floor_runner.rs), but --required is passed by 0 of the 9 files under .github/workflows and the only claim_executor invocation on a merge path is heal.yml:113 --regen-round-cost, with the manual floor runner deleted (gunbc.rung_drop_amendment.manual_required_floor_runner_deleted), so a roster row naming this control would be enrolled and never judged. main's control module (src/v2/test/claim/manual/annotation_gap_v1_v2_agreement.dag, 174 lines) names no rung and imports no admission census: grep for rung gives 0 hits and required_gate_admits gives 0. The sibling row this one splits from authors the rung and re-derives it from required_gate_admits(module_path:) in every arm; what no tree has is an executed subject that decides it. This row closes that gap, and it is not dispatchable until an executor exists to read the rung against.",
        displaced_cost: "While the control is ungated, a v1/v2 divergence in comment-attachment text is invisible to CI, and the control's green is only what an interactive claim_batch run says it is.",
        first_slice: "First the executor on a merge path, which this row depends on by its edge to row required-witness-floor-restored: a route that calls run_required_floor over the enrolled population on a landing revision, so the admission census has a subject instead of a roster alone. Then make the rung leg's refusal observable rather than merely true: with the control admitted to required_gate_authored_modules at the rung the sibling row publishes, its rung claim must be seen to refuse when the roster row is removed and to pass when it is restored, under one argv and one binary with the acquisition line printed. Only then is the control admitted to the gate.",
        red_control: "The rung claim has a PASS leg (probe_pair_rung_agrees_with_the_floors_own_admission, 989 eval steps, posted in the sibling row's PR) and NO observed refusal on any tree, which is why this row is NotDispatchable and why planning must brief no worker against it before the refusal is seen. Once an executor exists the first observation is that claim refusing a rung claimed as GatedRow while required_gate_admits admits src/v2/test/claim/manual/annotation_gap_v1_v2_agreement to no roster, printed with its identity block and its heavy-slot acquisition line; then, with that admission actually present in required_gate_authored_modules, the same claim must PASS under the same argv and the same binary, with the red_at and green_at commit shas and the carrier blob hashes printed. A PASS with no observed refusal, and a rung whose subject nothing executes, are not admission.",
        out_of_scope: "The consumer (parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2-consumed), the roster contents and the gap rule (parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2).",
        handback: "The agreement control runs under the required gate."
      )),
      dispatch: Present { value: NotDispatchable },
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-compiler-live", contribution: "The v2 compiler keeps authored prose under the same gap rule as the seed, and the agreement that rule holds is executed by the gate rather than by an interactive run."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_ZR45MIQ7KTC56XVLT23LXQ3W4A",
      id: "source-annotation-fold-carries-absorbed-capture-count",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/std/source_annotation.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The annotation fold states how many lex captures each annotation row absorbed, so a consumer need not re-count them",
        boundary: "Reported by vivid-swift-693 from tidy-fox-641's measurement during review 98412 on gunbc#16942; re-measure before closing, and cite by symbol per gunbc.design_argument::single_authority. std.source_annotation::SourceAnnotationDebt declares exactly four fields -- gap, text, origin, placement -- and carries no capture count, and v2.compiler.parse::parse_module_authored builds the parse's annotation rows as the product of std.source_annotation::attach_annotations over those same lex captures. So a consumer that wants to know how many captures a row absorbed cannot read it from the row, and the alternative it is offered -- compare the graph against a fold run -- compares a function with its own output and can never refuse anything. THE OBLIGATION EXISTS BECAUSE ITS ROW WAS RETIRED: parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2 (rn_EG37S52DZ2Q82J39S6JWZ88C36) closed as superseded by parse-comment-gap-attachment-v2-consumed, so no live row could hold it, and the consumer side carries a typed DissolutionCondition naming the field whose existence deletes its counter. That counter is the counter this row exists to make unnecessary: when the fold carries the count, the consumer's counter is dissolved by its own declared condition, in the consumer row's hands and not in this one. SCOPE, stated once and not contradicted below: what the annotation fold's PRODUCT carries -- the count of lex captures a row absorbed, and the single declaration that decides it. It is not which gap an annotation lands in, it takes no lane's feature work, it changes no gap rule, and it does not touch the consumer row's handback.",
        displaced_cost: "Every consumer of annotated parses that needs capture cardinality keeps its own counter, and each such counter is a second authority over a quantity the fold already decided, which is what a reviewer correctly objects to and what no same-source comparison can answer.",
        first_slice: "Report the measured count on main, then make the fold carry it: one added field on std.source_annotation::SourceAnnotationDebt and the one declaration that decides it in std.source_annotation::attach_annotations, shown by a witness that discriminates -- a source with a gap holding several lex captures is reported with that count and one holding none is reported without it, both legs in the same log. The consumer's own counter is OUT OF THIS ROW'S HANDS: it is deleted by its own declared DissolutionCondition, in the consumer row's hands, and this row's witnesses do not depend on that deletion.",
        red_control: "NOT YET OBSERVED, AND THE ROW IS NOT DISPATCHABLE UNTIL IT IS. The discriminating pair to be executed by the first worker or by planning, on main's released claim_batch, one binary and two trees: the WRONG case is an annotated fixture in which a fold absorbs more than one lex capture into an annotation row while the row reports no count, and the witness must REFUSE it; the RIGHT case is the same fixture once the count is carried, and the witness must ACCEPT it. Both legs print their own rc, the [witness] lines by name, the binary sha256 read from disk and the heavy-slot acquisition line. A row that only shows the accepted case is not a control; the refusal is what makes the count's absence detectable. Until that pair has been seen to fail on some tree this row stays NotDispatchable, because a control never observed red on any tree is not admission.",
        out_of_scope: "The annotation placement decision, which gap a row lands in, which capture the fold matches, any gap rule, a new parser or annotation kind, the consumer-side counter's deletion -- that happens by its own declared DissolutionCondition in the consumer row's hands and is NOT a deliverable here -- dag/std/source_annotation.dag's four well-formedness claims, and any pinned table or emission seed.",
        handback: "The added field and the single deciding declaration quoted verbatim from the tree that carries them, plus one executed log with raw PASS and FAIL lines and the runner's own rc, naming std.source_annotation::attach_annotations and std.source_annotation::SourceAnnotationDebt and the witness, printing the fold input and the reported count for both discriminating cases. It must show, by name with a count, that the well-formedness claims still present in std.source_annotation.dag were run unchanged -- not an empty grep. The consumer-side counter is reported as still present and untouched, because its deletion belongs to the consumer row: a handback that deletes it here is out of scope, and a handback that silently keeps it is correct."
      )),
      dispatch: Present { value: NotDispatchable },
      parent: in_project(project: "v2-compiler-live", contribution: "A parse's annotation rows say what they absorbed, so consumers read a decided quantity instead of re-deciding it."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_DCHDCNK5Z6GZP693VSZA6MC84T",
      id: "parse-prepared-grammar-analysis-carried",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/compiler/parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "The parser receives the grammar analysis validation already computed",
        boundary: "grammar_validate_and_analyze returns PreparedModeledGrammar { validated, analysis }; grammar_validate_for_parse keeps only validated and parse_production recomputes the analysis (compute_grammar_first_analysis wraps compute_grammar_first_analysis_given, which validation already called). Callers move onto the prepared result, the parse interface stops constructing analysis at all, and grammar_validate_for_parse plus the recompute are deleted. The migration carries TokenStream.layout through: the list-only prepared entry supplies absent layout where the stream route carries it.",
        displaced_cost: "Every parse through the validated door pays the FIRST analysis again, and a prepared grammar cannot be reused across files -- authored duplication gunbc.design_argument::demand_before_materialization says to carry or share, not cache.",
        first_slice: "parse_production takes the prepared grammar and one caller migrates.",
        red_control: "Prepare one grammar and parse at least two modules with that same prepared value: ONE full analysis across the whole operation, counted at the underlying _given work (a count of the wrapper alone already reads one on the defective path), or established structurally because no parse entry can construct analysis.",
        out_of_scope: "Speculative-parse rollback, which correctly discards failed attempts.",
        handback: "No caller of grammar_validate_for_parse remains, no parse entry constructs analysis, layout reaches every migrated caller, and the two-module control is green."
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11 from a review of the v2 parse interfaces: the discard is a gunbc.design_argument::minimize_redundancy conformance defect, not an optional optimisation.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "Removes authored duplicate work from every validated parse."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_BS0YK7QAA67P73F6YYGZRTGXJ3",
      id: "v1-parse-allocator-carried",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v1/02_parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "v1 parse carries its occurrence allocator instead of re-walking built subtrees",
        boundary: "v1 02_parse reconstructs allocator state by walking already-built subtrees (occurrence_allocator_after_node and siblings), including a traversal of the growing left operand in the binary-expression loop. Carry the allocator through accepted results and delete the recovery scans wherever that invariant holds.",
        displaced_cost: "Each re-walk repeats work proportional to the subtree, and the expression loop's is BETTED to grow quadratically in operand chain length -- an inference from its shape, carried as a bet and neither deleted nor promoted (gunbc.design_argument::epistemic_humility). The only measurement in hand reads linear at the sizes measured: the deletion saved 4493 eval steps at chain 40 and 22093 at chain 200, a ratio of 4.9 for a 5x chain, so no curve above linear has been observed and none has been refuted either. The bet is the shape to test when a counter exists; the cost gunbc.design_argument::how_to_work fixes regardless of today's n is the re-walk itself, which is measured, not its exponent.",
        first_slice: "The binary-expression loop carries the allocator from its operand result.",
        red_control: "ONE DISCRIMINATING PAIR, measured by planning on 2026-11-25 on srv1 under heavy-slot: one released claim_batch whose sha256 is a53867a57b587969e494b3989708ba741da2ad4ee7f316fb2a561a819e3afc6d (a binary hash, not a commit), one command line quoted verbatim and run identically at both trees with only the tree varying -- claim_batch --wet --source-root dag --source-root src/v1 --source-root src/v2 --entry src/v1/tests/claim/parse_allocator_postcondition_test.dag --functions chain200_transport_identity_matches_predeletion_golden, cwd the worktree root. THE CONTROL IS THE PAIR ITSELF, read off that run's own [witness] receipt line, emitted by `witness_report_line` in src/v1/stage0/src/bin/claim_batch.rs, whose own doc comment states eval_steps is the quantity the required floor enforces on. WRONG CASE REFUSED at red_at 5611ac47e730427f6d19f9bf765c561b01c36de7, where src/v1/02_parse.dag is sha256 c181dad5b764eaa1708a548ff39058879a4291644c3ac4ebcb83743136261326 at 7132 lines with occurrence_allocator_after_node at 14 hits: the line reads eval_steps=418120, above the bound. RIGHT CASE ACCEPTED at green_at 9eab2a2882a56571220b0d0b5d8d7864b7d8aafd, where that file is sha256 ee3e4a7dcdcdc32a9b4542ec139c85ebac18581d995657a57a62ae7f73801a83 at 7006 lines with 0 hits: the same line reads eval_steps=396027, below the bound. THE BOUND is B = 407000 eval steps and its provenance is the pair itself -- 396027 < 407000 < 418120 -- so it is a measured window and not a magic number. A legitimate change that costs steps will cross B; the answer is a fresh pair measured at two named shas on a named binary with B re-derived from the new window, never a silent widening of the literal. THE SHAPE DIFFERENTIAL that says the deletion moved it and not a global slowdown, same binary, same runs: chain200 content association 640998 to 618905, chain40 identity 88689 to 84196, chain40 content association 101218 to 96725, folds identity 48536 to 44896, folds content association 52937 to 49297 -- every delta tracks the chain, -22093 at length 200 and -4493 at length 40. WHAT THE BOUND IS NOT, stated so it is not read as the instrument the deleted sentence promised: it is a budget, not a proof of cause. It refuses when the chain-200 parse costs more steps and cannot say which walk cost them, and no counter reads the left-operand recovery walk -- occurrence_allocator_after_node has 0 hits in any claim module at main -- so the claim that would assert a zero re-walk count is unwritable today: a zero-count claim needs a counter, the counter is implementation, and it belongs to the follow-up named by gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_red_control_that_was_never_red_on_any_tree_reads_as_a_control. NO CLAIM ASSERTS B AND NONE IS NAMED AS THIS CONTROL: .dag cannot read its own step count -- `observed` in v2.test.floor_enrolment_margin builds its ObservedCpuReading as a literal the module passes itself, with the step count supplied by `fixture_incidental_steps` -- so a bound claim written there would return the same Bool at both trees and go green on whichever number a later author typed into the module, which is validation standing where construction was available and is refused here rather than planted. THE ENFORCEMENT, WHAT IS OBSERVABLE AND WHAT IS NOT: today the refusal is a READER -- the acceptance reviewer reading the two quoted [witness] lines against B -- and nothing in the host refuses either number. That is stated rather than dressed as a budget, because the floor cannot carry this quantity and this row promises it no longer. The per-identity budget carrier, `claim_eval_step_budget_for_identity` in v2.workflow.required_floor, can produce only two figures: `claim_ceiling_envelope_ms` gives 500 or 100 milliseconds of work and `eval_step_budget_for` multiplies it by `floor_eval_step_calibration`'s pinned 723 steps/ms, so a ceiling there is 361500 eval steps or 72300 -- and BOTH measured figures sit above the looser of them, which means a ceiling row would refuse the correct case along with the wrong one. The third route, `corpus_census_eval_step_allowance`, scales with a subject rather than bounding an identity and REFUSES any identity `corpus_census_roster` does not name, and this identity is not named: 0 hits for it anywhere under src/v2. Both measured figures sit ABOVE 361500, and the chain-200 identity has 0 hits anywhere under src/v2, so it is in none of those populations. A ceiling row for this identity is therefore not merely unwritten -- it is the WRONG INSTRUMENT: nothing in that file accepts 396027 while refusing 418120, so a future node wanting a machine check would add a refusal of its own green case, not a control. What is owed instead is stated once and in one place: cause-reading is rostered to the failure-mode row named above, whose follow-up is a counter over occurrence_allocator_after_node, and any future guard on the cost is a STRUCTURAL ASSERTION ON THE V1 SIDE in v1.tests.claim.parse_allocator_postcondition_test beside `chain200_transport_identity_matches_predeletion_golden`, which is where the fixture lives and where a step budget could be read by something that also builds it. This row does not edit v2.workflow.required_floor and routes nothing to it; the lanes this plan rosters as owners of that carrier are ci-cost and ci-control, and any future guard is a v1-side change in the claim module named above, whose owner this row already names as parse. The eight id-equality claims of the module are NOT this control either: the module is byte-identical at both trees (sha256 eb47ade80d3ba039ec4d4c551af3b841963a304ec4cb88b85610d49640e7dfdf) and all eight pass at both, so none was ever red with respect to the deletion.",
        out_of_scope: "Changing which ids are minted or their order.",
        handback: "Nine functions deleted from src/v1/02_parse.dag, occurrence_allocator_after_node at 0 hits (14 at red_at) and the file at 7006 lines (7132 at red_at); the eight claims of src/v1/tests/claim/parse_allocator_postcondition_test.dag green at green_at -- long_left_associated_chain_postcondition, parameters_arguments_statements_postcondition, chain40_transport_identity_matches_predeletion_golden, chain200_transport_identity_matches_predeletion_golden, folds_transport_identity_matches_predeletion_golden, chain40_transport_content_association_matches_predeletion_golden, chain200_transport_content_association_matches_predeletion_golden, folds_transport_content_association_matches_predeletion_golden; and the acceptance receipt's executed red control carrying red_at 5611ac47e730427f6d19f9bf765c561b01c36de7, green_at 9eab2a2882a56571220b0d0b5d8d7864b7d8aafd, the claim_batch binary sha256 a53867a57b587969e494b3989708ba741da2ad4ee7f316fb2a561a819e3afc6d, the command line, and both [witness] eval_steps receipt lines verbatim -- so B is read from the record rather than asserted, and the eight guards arrive as regression guards and not as the control.",
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11 from the parser recomputation audit. A source-level finding, not a measured speedup."), on: "2026-11-25", note: "Rewritten 2026-11-25 (planning, eager-bear-102, after the lane manager's ruling on the implementation worker's answer). The 2026-10-11 red_control named no executable witness and promised an instrument that does not exist; the claims it was read against are byte-identical at main and at the implementation head and pass at both, so no control was ever observed red on any tree. The pair quoted in red_control was measured for this rewrite on srv1 under heavy-slot, one binary, two trees. The counter that would prove cause is not written here; it is rostered to the failure-mode row named in red_control. The bound-claim shape first drafted here (a .dag claim taking its own observed reading as a parameter) is struck and refused in red_control, because the reading would be a literal the module passes itself, and the enforcement sentence is recorded honestly rather than as a promise: the required floor cannot carry this quantity (its two derived budgets are 361500 and 72300 eval steps and the identity is in neither population), so the refusal today is a reader of the quoted pair, cause-reading is rostered to the failure-mode row, and a future cost guard belongs on the v1 side in the claim module beside the fixture, not as a ceiling row in v2.workflow.required_floor.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "Removes the occurrence-allocator re-walk from the seed parser; its measured cost is the saved eval steps, its quadratic growth in chain length is this row's bet, not a measured curve."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_HQF67ZYAVPCEVTYNYWMCC6N5KM",
      id: "v2-grammar-preparation-first-summaries",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/compiler/parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "v2 grammar preparation keeps each child's FIRST summary instead of rebuilding it",
        boundary: "Prepared child construction drops child FIRST summaries and later reconstructs them, repeating FIRST-union and guard-summary work within preparation. Retain the child summaries in the prepared expression and consume them. Separate from parse-prepared-grammar-analysis-carried, which fixes the validation-to-parse handoff, not repetition inside preparation.",
        displaced_cost: "Every grammar preparation repeats summary work per child.",
        first_slice: "One choice node consumes its children's retained summaries.",
        red_control: "Prepared output is identical before and after on the .dag grammar, and an instrument shows each child summary built once.",
        out_of_scope: "The validation-to-parse handoff.",
        handback: "No reconstruction of a child summary remains in preparation."
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11 from the parser recomputation audit. A source-level finding, not a measured speedup.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "Removes repeated FIRST computation from grammar preparation."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_D48CRKRR0HAT5PNPTVY491JD05",
      id: "v2-predicate-recognition-without-materialization",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/compiler/parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "v2 predicates recognize without materializing nodes they then discard",
        boundary: "A predicate path can materialize a terminal (minting and recording it), discard it, and the accepted infix path then materializes it again. Recognition during predicates avoids unnecessary materialization while preserving refusal and layout behavior. Necessary speculative rollback of a failed attempt is correct and stays.",
        displaced_cost: "Work spent minting and recording nodes whose only purpose was a yes/no answer.",
        first_slice: "The infix continuation guard recognizes without minting.",
        red_control: "Parse trees, refusals and layout decisions identical on the corpus, and the predicate path mints no occurrence.",
        out_of_scope: "Speculative rollback of failed alternatives.",
        handback: "No predicate path materializes a node it discards."
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11 from the parser recomputation audit. A source-level finding, not a measured speedup.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "Removes discarded materialization from predicate recognition."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_A8SREB643YJ2KHT9424TXFE388",
      id: "v1-source-chars-converted-once",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v1/01_tokenize.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "v1 converts a source to characters once for tokenizing and newline indexing",
        boundary: "v1 tokenization and newline-index construction each convert the same source text to characters. Building the newline index is useful work; converting the source again is the repeated part. Convert once and hand the result to both.",
        displaced_cost: "Every ingested file pays the conversion twice.",
        first_slice: "The newline index consumes the tokenizer's converted characters.",
        red_control: "Tokens and newline indices identical before and after on the corpus, with one conversion per file.",
        out_of_scope: "Changing token or index semantics.",
        handback: "One conversion per source file on the ingest path."
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11 from the parser recomputation audit. A source-level finding, not a measured speedup.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "Removes a repeated per-file conversion from ingest."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_FA2BX92WF9PGE7FWZBG60E0XP8",
      id: "v2-parse-final-token-retained",
      owner: "parse",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/compiler/parse.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "v2 parse keeps the final token its order check already holds for the end-of-input diagnostic",
        boundary: "Token-order validation holds the final token and discards it; the end-of-input diagnostic later recovers it. Carry it in the consumption state instead. A small cold-path item, deferred behind parse-node-source-extent, whose consumption state it may share.",
        displaced_cost: "A cold-path re-derivation of a value already held.",
        first_slice: "The EOF diagnostic reads the retained final token.",
        red_control: "EOF diagnostics identical before and after; the recovery walk is gone.",
        out_of_scope: "Other diagnostics.",
        handback: "The recovery walk deleted."
      ), on: "2026-10-11", note: "Authored 2026-10-11 from the parser recomputation audit. A source-level finding, not a measured speedup.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "Removes a re-derivation of the final token."),
    ),

    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_RSPTW7T9VRES9F7FHCP08QFGX5",
      id: "ci-required-run-cost",
      owner: "ci-cost",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/workflow/required_floor.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "Make the required run cost what its inputs require, measured on the run that owns the number",
        boundary: "The required job is v2-bootstrap, which builds the seed and runs one gunbc test step per row of gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate emitted_subject_build_rows; no job runs claim_executor --required-ci (gunbc.rung_drop v1_required_lanes_withdrawn), so no phase or [floor-phase] receipt exists to read; the runner's modules remain as source but have no caller on an executing route. Its cost is read from that job's own step timings, never transcribed. Work under this row attributes wall time to a step by instrument, then removes computation whose inputs did not change, one step at a time, with the attribution re-read from the run after each change.",
        displaced_cost: "Every PR waits for the whole run (about thirty minutes at the 2026-08-22 measurement, from the run named in gunbc.design_document::ci_contract_blocks); the fifteen rows this replaces optimised a plan, a scheduler and a scoped worker that no longer exist, so they could only be closed by re-creating what the floor cut deleted.",
        first_slice: "One row per emitted-subject step (the rows of emitted_subject_build_rows, two today) in a receipt the job emits: step label, wall time, and the subject size it ran over; the seed build and other non-row steps are outside this receipt unless their population is derived from the required workflow model, so a later change can be judged against the same instrument rather than a remembered number.",
        red_control: "A change that claims to cut a step's time without the job's own receipt moving refuses review; a receipt whose step list differs from the rows of emitted_subject_build_rows (the emitted-subject steps only) is a defect, not a report.",
        out_of_scope: "No return of batches, plan entries, selection flags or a second worker process; no corpus-denominated fallback; the tokenizer escape-scan row stays separate.",
        handback: "The per-step receipt on the required job, and the first step attribution read from it."
      ), on: "2026-09-30", note: "Not yet centered: needs a centering plan: handed off from the v2 worksheet to the CI lane with no plan.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "ci-end-to-end-execution", contribution: "The required run is paid on every landing; attributing and cutting its cost keeps verification affordable enough to stay required."),
    ),

