    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_E4T64NMJ992QHGRWCCV9AS0J7M",
      id: "dp-m6-native-route-walls",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/rung_drop.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: level_fields(
          headline: "DP-M6: v2 native-route walls restored",
          boundary: "The native-route rung drops retired and the eval-step drop family shrunk. Of the three drops the census named (g0_type_decl_modifier_parse_without_sealing_property, variant_fields_unlowered_on_the_native_route, admit_callers_discarded_on_the_native_route), admit_callers_discarded_on_the_native_route is retired by its trigger (v2.compiler.infer infer_caller_admission_diags) and g0_type_decl_modifier_parse_without_sealing_property is retired by its trigger -- the native route consumes NormalizedTree type_declaration_modifiers and refuses foreign construction of a sole_constructor type (v2.compiler.infer infer_sole_constructor_diags), keyed on the declaring module. parse_tree_to_target_model_bridge and cross_language_compile infer CoreNode with an empty declaration index and a covering-no-modules channel: they are not `.dag` compile routes and cannot carry a `.dag` sole_constructor type (enrolled fixtures are anonymous_signature_arrow; the wall never consults). variant_fields_unlowered_on_the_native_route remains.",
          approach: "Re-derive the population from gunbc.rung_drop, then one drop per change with its RED fixture and positive control.",
          red_control: "A drop retired without its restoration capability executing keeps this open (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder (no silent regression)).",
          displaced_cost: "Each standing drop is a known wall down on the path v2 is meant to run on.",
          handback: "The drops retired, named by their restoring witnesses."
        )),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "Rung drops on the native route are declared safety regressions with a finite runway; restoring them returns the route to its prior rung."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_NNZV6UA6MA76X6P719W9QCO1Z3",
      id: "dp-m2-codex-direct-argv-policy",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/codex_app_server_press.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: level_fields(
          headline: "DP-M2: the Codex trip stops paying an SSH wall's price at a direct-exec seam",
          boundary: "One authority transition: the Codex-trip arm stops admitting CODEX_HOME through the SSH portable-word wall and is bounded instead by what its own seam owes. Measured on main: codex_press_account_trip_argv_admission (dag/gunbc/codex_app_server_press.dag) admits the words through gunbc.typed_argv_exec::typed_argv_shell_safe_refusal_argv (dag/gunbc/typed_argv_exec.dag) -- a wall admitting only [A-Za-z0-9._-/@:=] whose own commentary (typed_argv_exec.dag) justifies it by the ssh remote half, where exec carries a command STRING the remote user's shell re-parses (the deployment argument gunbc.remote_shell_command carries in its own commentary: RFC 4254 section 6.5 gives the exec request one string-typed command field and does not mandate a shell, while OpenSSH's sshd runs it through the target user's login shell), whose refusal diagnostic reads ssh remote argv is shell-reinterpreted, and which states that the portable-word check is not duplicated here because typed_argv_shell_safe_refusal_argv exists at the SshShell seam. The Codex route has no such string and no ssh: the module never names ssh, and the admitted words go to extdeps.shell.exec::RunArgvStdin (dag/extdeps/shell/exec.dag), whose transport is argv: [program, arguments] with stdin_payload -- no shell, no word splitting -- reached from codex_observed_standing_for_instance (codex_app_server_press.dag) and observe_codex_account_preflight . The shell route is the OTHER operation: extdeps.shell.exec::Run is the one that ships bash -s on stdin . So refusing a semicolon or a space at the Codex seam restricts which pathnames may be named; it prevents no injection. The Codex boundary's witness checks argv structure and value preservation -- an adversarial CODEX_HOME carrying a semicolon and one carrying a space each arrive as ONE argv value with their bytes intact, with no shell run -- and checks that the SSH portable-word wall is not applied at a direct-exec seam. Named consumers of the transition: the two production callers above, which hand Admitted.arguments straight to RunArgvStdin. A restricted Codex-home alphabet, if anyone wants one, needs its own authority and its own rationale, not a borrowed SSH wall.",
          approach: "1-3 files, 50-150 changed lines, one red-on-base/green-on-head claim. Files: dag/gunbc/codex_app_server_press.dag (codex_press_account_trip_argv_admission, its wall call, and the commentary above the arm naming the wall the trip's single shell-safety authority), dag/test/claim/codex_app_server_press_witness.dag (the arm codex_press_account_trip_admission_refuses_adversarial_codex_home, which asserts CodexTripArgvRefused on /tmp/codex-home; reboot), dag/test/claim/shell/shell_dag_codex_app_server_trip_script_witness.dag (adversarial_codex_home_renders_no_shell_control_word, same value). Both witnesses already plant that value and today pin the refusal; this node re-cuts them to pin structure and preservation instead, on the dry claim route that already executes them (the roots of record are gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots::witness_discovery_scan_dirs, declared in dag/gunbc/ci/ci_layer_roots.dag, executed by .github/workflows/witnesses.yml). For scale: the PR that introduced this shape, gunbc#16536 (fe606a8ed62b), was 7 files and 250 insertions over 101 deletions because it re-cut five sibling arms too; this node is the residue, so 3 files is the ceiling. No new wall is authored here and no alphabet is introduced -- a wanted Codex-home restriction is a separate node with its own rationale, per the source-annotation rule that a check must say what it is for.",
          red_control: "A CODEX_HOME carrying a semicolon or a space refused at the direct-exec seam keeps this open: the structure-and-preservation claim is red on base, where both named witnesses still expect CodexTripArgvRefused, and turns green only when the adversarial value reaches RunArgvStdin as one preserved argv element.",
          displaced_cost: "Every Codex home outside [A-Za-z0-9._-/@:=] is unadministrable on this route today -- a space or a semicolon in a path is a legal and ordinary filename -- and the refusal buys no injection safety at a seam that passes argv structurally, while the borrowed wall's ssh commentary misstates what the check is for, so the next reader cannot tell a pathname rule from an injection rule.",
          handback: "The two named witnesses execute red-on-base and green-on-head on the dry claim route: the adversarial CODEX_HOME arrives as ONE argv value with its bytes intact, no shell runs on the route, and no direct-exec seam in the Codex path calls the SSH portable-word wall."
        )),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "DP-M2 retired shell residue by moving spawns onto typed argv; one wall that change left standing is an SSH wall quoted at a direct-exec seam, where it can only restrict pathnames."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_01M7NQG5SWMS74ZGXDH6X55TQ8",
      id: "extdeps-subject-ref-facts-host-census",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/std/decl_index.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
          headline: "extdeps-subject-ref-facts: a host census reads an ExternalModelScope data declaration's subject as typed fields, so DP-M3b has subjects to resolve",
          boundary: "A keyed decl_facts_at node (src/v2/std/decl_index.dag decl_facts_at) over an ExternalModelScope exposes only flat authored_string_lexemes: there is no subject/declaration/module_path field edge and record_field_projection is Absent, so a typed subject read cannot be built from it. Root cause measured by the DP-M3b worker (bright-wolf-535, PR #16609): marshal_decl_fact_node(DataItem) routes through marshal_data_initializer_projection, ctx.lookup_fn_node is None for extdeps scopes never loaded into the interpreter, so it falls to constructor_resolution_refused_projection, and marshal_plain_record_projection fails closed unless the type resolves in the importing module's type env. The existing resolver (src/v2/std/decl_ref_resolution_host.dag: v2.std.decl_ref_resolution_host, resolve_declaration_ref_live, declaration_ref_resolution_census_live) takes refs as input and discovers no subjects. The operator ruled DP-M3b option A on 2026-11-17 (A is fine), and the worker measured that A cannot be built inside DP-M3b. So a typed subject read needs a NEW host builtin (working name extdeps_subject_ref_facts): a BuiltinSignature added to the builtin_function_registry of src/v1/04_method.dag (type BuiltinSignature), which regenerates src/v1/stage0/src/v1_compiler_infer_method.rs; a primitive_identity_slug entry added to the roster of dag/std/primitive_projection.dag, which regenerates src/v1/stage0/src/std_primitive_projection.rs; the dispatch roster entry, which regenerates src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated.rs; an eval in src/v1/stage0/src/coproduct_reflection.rs cloned from eval_data_decl_type_facts over decls_parse_only_fail_closed; the census data type and fn in src/v2/std/decl_index.dag beside DataDeclTypeFact and data_decl_type_facts; and a SeedGrowthJustification for the new seed. The three generated files are regenerated by the v1 compiler and never hand-edited; the dispatch bijection witnesses stay green: test.claim.v1_interpreter_primitive_dispatch_authority_acceptance, the surface witness in dag/gunbc/v1/v1_interpreter_primitive_surface, and the roster witness in test.claim.interpreter_dispatch_bijection_real_roster_witness.",
          displaced_cost: "Without this census DP-M3b stays blocked: option A hands the resolver typed subject.declaration \{module_path, decl_name} values, but the only read available over an ExternalModelScope today is the untyped, flat authored_string_lexemes. Left undone, a reader guesses the subject positionally -- the failure mode gunbc.bare_name_resolved_by_proximity_standing_in_for_resolution refuses -- and the DP-M3 milestone's consumer half stalls with the 844 migrated subject refs never reaching the resolver that already exists.",
          first_slice: "Declare the builtin end to end without hand-editing any generated file: add the BuiltinSignature to builtin_function_registry in src/v1/04_method.dag, the primitive_identity_slug entry in dag/std/primitive_projection.dag, the eval in src/v1/stage0/src/coproduct_reflection.rs cloned from eval_data_decl_type_facts, and the census data type plus fn in src/v2/std/decl_index.dag beside data_decl_type_facts; attach a SeedGrowthJustification (gunbc.seed_growth::SeedGrowthJustification) whose hand_authored_declarations is the seed census -- the new eval_extdeps_subject_ref_facts fn in src/v1/stage0/src/coproduct_reflection.rs cloned from eval_data_decl_type_facts, its BuiltinSignature in src/v1/04_method.dag, its primitive identity in dag/std/primitive_projection.dag, and its census type plus fn in src/v2/std/decl_index.dag (the three generated files are regenerated, not hand-authored rows); a reason; an owning_dissolution_lane; and a trigger, so the hand-written eval is a declared row and never a silent escape hatch per gunbc.design_argument::self_hosting; the trigger must name the condition that deletes the eval: an emitted v2 fold resolves an ExternalModelScope data declaration's subject in-DAG, i.e. marshal_data_initializer_projection no longer falls to constructor_resolution_refused_projection for extdeps scopes because the scope is loaded so ctx.lookup_fn_node resolves the subject type in the importing module's type env and record_field_projection is Present -- at which point the eval and its roster rows are deleted; regenerate src/v1/stage0/src/v1_compiler_infer_method.rs, src/v1/stage0/src/std_primitive_projection.rs and src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated.rs through the v1 compiler; then land one claim that reads one ExternalModelScope data declaration and returns subject.declaration \{module_path, decl_name} as typed fields, red on base and green on head.",
          red_control: "For an ExternalModelScope data declaration the census returns subject.declaration \{module_path, decl_name} as typed fields (a typed read of both), a non-literal subject row REFUSES rather than being guessed positionally, and the three generated files match the v1 compiler's regeneration with the dispatch bijection witnesses (authority, surface and roster) holding.",
          out_of_scope: "This is the declaration of the census node only; no implementation lands in this plan PR. Resolution belongs to dp-m3-subject-ref-resolved, which consumes the typed subjects this census discovers -- this node discovers and types them and resolves nothing. The three generated files are never hand-edited, no subject resolution is added to the census, and DP-M3b's own ticket text is left untouched; the only change to it is the new dependency edge.",
          handback: "Return the census builtin reading an ExternalModelScope data declaration's subject.declaration \{module_path, decl_name} as typed fields, the refusal on a non-literal subject row, the three regenerated files with the authority, surface and roster bijection witnesses green, and the SeedGrowthJustification -- the prerequisite dp-m3-subject-ref-resolved then resolves against."
        )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "DP-M3b's option A read of typed subject refs cannot be built inside DP-M3b: the only read over an ExternalModelScope is the untyped authored_string_lexemes, so a host census must first return subject.declaration \{module_path, decl_name} as typed fields for the existing resolver to consume."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_H3NLOAANZ5KMIP89PZHKFN2N5A",
      id: "dp-m3-subject-ref-resolved",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/extdeps_scope_frontier.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: level_fields(
          headline: "DP-M3: the 844 subject refs the drain migrated meet the resolver that already exists",
          boundary: "One authority transition: an ExternalSubjectRef stops being a value that is compared and becomes a reference that resolves. Measured on main: 833 files under dag/ and src/ carry 844 rows of subject: ExternalSubjectRef (grep over the tree) -- the type is extdeps.external_authority::ExternalSubjectRef (dag/extdeps/external_authority.dag), holding a DeclarationRef of module_path plus decl_name -- and 859 files declare extdeps_model_scope. Nothing asks whether the named module declares the named thing. The model says so in its own words (dag/gunbc/extdeps_scope_frontier.dag, WHAT THE RULE DOES NOT BUY): nothing resolves an ExternalSubjectRef module_path/decl_name against the declarations of the module it names, the claim corpus reads the field only as a value, and String offers string_contains and split but no substring and no index, so a text scan cannot bound a name. Its only consumer today compares hand-typed values: test.claim.external_model_scope_witness::adopted_vendor_scopes_coherent_by_execution (dag/test/claim/external_model_scope_witness.dag) compares two literals through extdeps.external_authority::external_subject_ref_eq, and the module's other 29 claims build synthetic refs (fn subject, fn synthetic_scope) or check carrier text. The boundary: the resolver that already exists is applied to that population -- v2.std.decl_ref_resolution_host::resolve_declaration_ref_live and ::declaration_ref_resolution_census_live (src/v2/std/decl_ref_resolution_host.dag), built on the containment-tree module census rather than a text scan, with typed arms ModuleAbsent, DeclarationAbsent, FieldAbsent and Ambiguous -- refusing a name the declaring module does not declare, a prefix-only service match (spelling cron where the module declares service cron.Tab, the frontier's own named failure), a reference to a module that is neither the carrier nor its sanctioned subject-carrier sibling, and a name declared twice. It executes over the full 844-row population. No second checker is written: 33 .dag files already import this resolver, including dag/test/claim/long/decl_ref_resolution_witness.dag and dag/test/claim/long/carrier_reference_integrity_witness.dag, which resolve named carriers red-on-base.",
          approach: "3-6 files, 50-150 changed lines, one red-on-base/green-on-head claim, host grain. Measured reasons for that grain, so the node does not repeat a dead end: the full-population parse-grain walk in claims is OFFLINE by operator decision -- src/v2/test/claim/long/mandatory_tag_extdeps_corpus.dag is excluded from CI discovery by gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots::witness_exclusion_substrings (dag/gunbc/ci/ci_layer_roots.dag) because the interpreted parse measured ~33s per module on 2026-07-22 -- and the construction door cannot carry it either, because v2.compiler.compile::required_compile_lens_roster (src/v2/compiler/compile.dag) admits only a pure fold over the tree being compiled, which cannot see a sanctioned subject-carrier sibling's declarations. So the consumer lands at the census grain the resolver was minted for, joining the consumers it already has -- dag/gunbc/generated_artifact_producer_coverage.dag, dag/gunbc/integration_concern.dag, src/v2/lens/common/outside_modeled_guarantee_join.dag and the claim witnesses dag/test/claim/long/decl_ref_resolution_witness.dag, dag/test/claim/long/carrier_reference_integrity_witness.dag, dag/test/claim/fleet_naming_witness.dag and dag/test/claim/decl_ref_keyed_read_agreement_witness.dag -- and NOT as a new corpus-wide CI job: gunbc.rung_drop.cited_symbol_census declares that very check retired (declared 2026-08-23, standing Retired with trigger fired 2026-08-25), so a second corpus-wide job would resurrect a rung the model has already dropped. The carrier population is enumerated from the same walk the census already runs; the four phantom arms are planted as red controls. ZERO bulk carrier migration in this node: no carrier file is edited and the legacy manifest stays at its measured 176 rows (wc -l on dag/gunbc/legacy_extdeps_scope_frontier.tsv, halved from 352 by gunbc#16511, c7efe927c4f6) -- no further drain runs until this consumer executes.",
          red_control: "The four phantom classes, each planted and each green on base under every existing witness because all of them read the row as text or as a value: a name the target module never declared; a prefix-only service match; a reference to a module that is neither the carrier nor a sanctioned subject-carrier sibling; a name declared twice in the target module. Each must refuse under the resolver.",
          displaced_cost: "844 rows read as an authority claim and are checked as a string, so the model's subject story rests on rows nobody reads: a phantom costs one editing keystroke, the drain cannot be re-run against any criterion, and every further drain PR pays the same hand re-verification cost with no consumer to amortize it against.",
          handback: "One executing consumer over all 844 subject rows on the census route the resolver already runs, green on main and red on each of the four planted phantoms, with the manifest unmoved at 176 rows by this node."
        )),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "DP-M3 halved the legacy scope manifest and enrolled its gate; the subjects that migration moved are still storage-grain, so the second half of that milestone is the consumer that reads them."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_0HLUS1PYYH39HTYZ7O5PDAEZOB",
      id: "dp-m5-consumer-family-converted",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/roadmap/attempt_system_unit_launch.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: level_fields(
          headline: "DP-M5: the attempt-spool read family is converted onto the typed read fold",
          boundary: "DP-M5 has been measurement only: gunbc#16496 (2eefc8a1fe64) moved one line of prose in the adoption standing, and gunbc#16509 (1a923810e054) enrolled two witnesses by name in v2.workflow.required_floor -- no production consumer was converted. This node converts ONE cohesive family: the four identities that read the attempt spool and the worktree's git pointers under the instance root, all projecting raw std.resources Filesystem scalars outside extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io::filesystem_read_outcome (dag/extdeps/filesystem/filesystem_io.dag, whose FilesystemReadOutcome already separates Read, Absent and CouldNotLook). Measured, with each collapse named: gunbc.roadmap.attempt_system_unit_launch::attempt_system_unit_read_answer (dag/gunbc/roadmap/attempt_system_unit_launch.dag) maps !read.success to AttemptSystemUnitAnswerMissing, which attempt_system_unit_harness_start_from_observation reads as Pending -- so an unreadable answer keeps an attempt waiting on a reply it cannot see, while the domain already carries AttemptSystemUnitAnswerUnreadable { detail } reserved for decode failure; ::attempt_system_unit_read_claimed_body drops read.error into the fixed reason claimed request body unreadable while the sibling stat fold beside it carries its cause; gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_belt_actuate::belt_prior_attempt_recovery (dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_belt_actuate.dag) takes the else arm to SessionRecoveryNone, so an unreadable attempt log reads as no prior session to recover; ::belt_commit_git_trust collapses two reads into the prose causes worktree gitfile is missing or unreadable and worktree commondir is missing or unreadable, with no read.error, on the route belt_commit_git uses to decide committer identity -- an untrusted trust decides ExecRefused . Each is converted onto filesystem_read_outcome or that module's typed domain fold, with a could-not-look control per converted site whose absent arm takes a different action.",
          approach: "One family, four identities, two production files plus their claim witnesses: 3-6 files, 50-150 changed lines, one red-on-base/green-on-head claim. Production: dag/gunbc/roadmap/attempt_system_unit_launch.dag (2 Filesystem.Read sites) and dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_belt_actuate.dag (9 of them; 14 files in that directory hold sites, and this node touches none of the others). Witnesses: dag/test/claim/roadmap/attempt_system_unit_launch_witness.dag (68 test fns) and dag/test/claim/roadmap/roadmap_belt_actuate_witness.dag (150), where the four could-not-look claims are enrolled. The executing route is the dry claim corpus -- the roots of record are gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots::witness_discovery_scan_dirs (dag/gunbc/ci/ci_layer_roots.dag: dag/test/claim, src/v2/test/claim/manual, src/v2/test/claim/emit), executed by .github/workflows/witnesses.yml -- the same route gunbc#16509 used. Two of the sites are today reached only from wet witnesses (dag/test/claim/roadmap/dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness.dag, dag/test/claim/roadmap/roadmap_belt_recapture_wet_witness.dag) and one, belt_prior_attempt_recovery, is reached from NO witness at all; wet is not a dispatcher -- no file under .github/workflows passes --wet -- so the four new claims must be dry claims on that route, not additions to the wet ones. Success is a smaller writable state space at these four sites, not an updated count in the standing.",
          red_control: "A converted site where an unreadable path still reads as absent: the answer file that exists but cannot be read reports Pending, the recovery reports SessionRecoveryNone, the claimed-body refusal still cannot tell unreadable from absent, or the gitfile refusal still says missing or unreadable in one arm. Red on base at each of the four sites, green on head.",
          displaced_cost: "The standing keeps its prose count while these four sites keep their conflation: a launcher or belt that cannot read a spool artifact takes the absent path's action, which for an attempt means waiting on an answer that may already be written, or launching a replacement for a session that may already exist, and no check can see the difference.",
          handback: "Four identities converted, each with its own could-not-look claim, executing red-on-base and green-on-head on the dry claim route."
        )),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "DP-M5 established absence as an established fact and enrolled its witnesses; a family of production consumers still folds could-not-look into absent, and this node converts that family."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_LBPUUNXDZTY1HATA9CE7H6YF19",
      id: "debt-grandfathered-fold-enrolled",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/workflow/floor_grandfathered_roster.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: level_fields(
          headline: "Debt: the grandfathered-removals fold is enrolled on a route that runs",
          boundary: "One authority transition: the fold gunbc#16506 (97d819858c5b) shipped stops being a model with fixtures and becomes a gate on an executing route. src/v2/workflow/floor_grandfathered_roster.dag (2,841 lines) declares type GrandfatheredRemoval, data floor_grandfathered_removals, fn floor_grandfathered_holds, fn removal_withdraws, fn roster_change_verdict, fn first_undisposed_removal and fn roster_change_admitted ; the module's own commentary says an addition is refused by roster_change_verdict -- NOTHING ON THE ACCEPTANCE PATH CALLS IT -- and v2.workflow.required_floor repeats it. Measured consumer set: the grep for these folds outside the declaring module returns ONE file, dag/test/claim/floor_eval_step_budget_witness.dag (26 test fns), whose five verdict calls all pass planted three-row lists, and whose own comment says exactly that -- fixtures rather than folding the live roster; no .rs file anywhere mentions grandfathered. The boundary: THAT fold, not a second checker, is called on a route that runs today over the live roster (the cut behind floor_grandfathered_cut_size, which that witness only asserts is above 3000 rows) and the live removal rows, so a change that adds a hold or leaves a removal undisposed refuses where it is today admitted. Measured route reality, so the node enrolls on something that runs: the wet schedule is not a route -- v2.workflow.local_repo_wet_terminal::local_repo_wet_schedule (src/v2/workflow/local_repo_wet_terminal.dag) has no dispatcher, its only host reader is src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/required_floor_runner.rs which validates the rows and refuses any expectation but ExpectedToHold (the refusal text: has no executor arm in this lane; only ExpectedToHold is realizable today), and no file under .github/workflows passes --wet. The route that does run is the dry claim corpus .github/workflows/witnesses.yml executes on every pull request, which already runs those 26 claims from the roots of gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots::witness_discovery_scan_dirs.",
          approach: "1-3 files, 50-150 changed lines, one red-on-base/green-on-head claim: the existing fold gains its executing consumer inside src/v2/workflow/floor_grandfathered_roster.dag and dag/test/claim/floor_eval_step_budget_witness.dag, where the fixtures already reach it and the live cut and removal rows are reachable on the same route. Do not write a second checker -- the frontier is declared in this same module and naming another one is the failure mode this program keeps hitting. Do not enroll on the wet schedule: measured above, it has no dispatcher. If no executing route can carry the live-roster claim, the honest handback is that there is none, with that recorded where the verdict is declared rather than a schedule row invented to look like one.",
          red_control: "A removal row on the live roster with no disposition, or a hold added with no removal behind it, leaves every existing check green today because only planted three-row fixtures reach the verdict; after enrollment the same state refuses on the executing claim, red on base and green on head.",
          displaced_cost: "A roster of exempted witness rows whose removal is modelled but never consulted: the model says an undisposed removal is refused, no route asks the question, and so the one file that decides which stale rows stay exempted can drift in exactly the direction the fold was written to stop.",
          handback: "One executing claim over the live roster, calling the fold #16506 already shipped, red on base and green on head."
        )),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "The debt batch that declared the grandfathered removals shipped the verdict and its fixtures; the acceptance path never calls it, so the roster's one real invariant is currently unenforced."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_AG04P6UEWDXRCNG3OWIC6M5PQF",
      id: "dp-m4-discovery-floor-semantic",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/non_fold_residue.rs"),
      line: ticket(t: level_fields(
          headline: "DP-M4: the discovery floor says what discovery must find, not how many rows",
          boundary: "One authority transition: the discovery-health criterion stops being a count a payoff PR may move and becomes a statement of what discovery must find. Measured on main: the criterion is entries.len() >= 64 in src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs, inside the host test nfr_live_union_is_populated_unique_and_tripled), reading super::non_fold_residue_roster_entries() (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/non_fold_residue.rs), which walks dag/ and src/v2/ for the .dag files declaring data non_fold_residue_frontier and keys each row as the carrier path joined to its declared subject path. The DP-M4 payoff PR that retired rows also moved this criterion: commit 922b0efb4780 (gunbc#16499, slice H) changed that same line from >= 100 to >= 64, so the measurement was renegotiated inside the change it measures. Measured population today: 99 rows over 42 carriers -- 56 rows across 24 carriers under dag/, 43 across 18 under src/v2/ -- leaving 35 rows of slack under a floor that has no relation to what discovery is for. The replacement names the population instead: every rostered row resolves to a declaration the module it names actually declares (my scan of main: all 99 resolve -- 91 bare rows joining their own carrier, 8 spelled as another module's path with the declaration present there), and every file under the two roots that declares the frontier contributes its rows to the union -- so neither a stale row nor a subtree the walk has stopped reaching can pass. Named consumers of both sides: non_fold_residue_site_is_rostered, which the complexity-linearity audit asks per wildcard site in src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs and which the refusal path asks -- so the same roster already decides a claim-visible fact.",
          approach: "1-3 files, 50-150 changed lines, one red-on-base/green-on-head claim. Files: src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs (replace the count assertion inside nfr_live_union_is_populated_unique_and_tripled), src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/non_fold_residue.rs (the resolution and carrier-completeness folds, beside the walk that already parses every carrier and the rostered fact), and at most one .dag claim module if the two properties are better witnessed there than in the host test. The route that runs today is the cargo test route that assertion already sits on, and the two semantic properties are asked there and nowhere else. No count is reintroduced at any threshold, and no new schedule, roster or CI job is created to hold the check.",
          red_control: "A discovery regression the count floor misses and the semantic check catches: rename a rostered declaration without retiring its row (the union keeps its 99 rows, so the floor stays green while a row points at nothing), or drop the src/v2/compiler carriers from the walk (29 rows over 9 carriers go missing and 70 remain, still over 64). Both are green on base; both refuse on head.",
          displaced_cost: "While the criterion is a number, any payoff PR that legitimately retires rows must edit the criterion -- which is exactly what slice H did -- so the measurement stopped measuring and the floor became a figure negotiated with the corpus instead of a claim about it; and any population above the floor passes, however wrong its rows.",
          handback: "The count floor is gone. The two named semantic properties execute on the route that assertion ran on, are green on main at 99 rows over 42 carriers, and refuse on each planted regression."
        )),
      parent: in_project(project: "debt-payoff", contribution: "DP-M4's payoff PRs retired frontier rows and, in one of them, moved the floor that measures whether the frontier is still being discovered; this node replaces that criterion with one the corpus cannot be negotiated against."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_QUMPMHS49PJ96ARB5LM6YLVUHF",
      id: "lab-standings-track-main",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/release_binding.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
          headline: "The lab's standings serve origin/main, with source and binary advancing as one pair",
          boundary: "One authority transition: the srv1 lab stops serving a plan from a hand-pinned checkout under an unpaired binary, and serves only under a release binding that holds. Measured on main, the refresh is already model-emitted -- gunbc.live_deploy.emit::live_deploy_belt_standings_refresh_unit_file (dag/gunbc/live_deploy/emit.dag, which runs belt_observe_once_cli) and ::live_deploy_belt_standings_refresh_timer_unit_file, whose OnBootSec and OnUnitInactiveSec are gunbc.live_deploy.belt_cadence::belt_standings_refresh_backstop (dag/gunbc/live_deploy/belt_cadence.dag: 600 seconds) with AccuracySec at the tick cadence, and whose staleness gate derives belt_standings_refresh_staleness_horizon = 2 * backstop + belt_tick_run_bound because the fold itself measures 13-16 minutes with a 2700-second ceiling (emit.dag). The agreement authority already exists and is already gated: gunbc.live_deploy.release_binding::DeployedReleaseBinding (dag/gunbc/live_deploy/release_binding.dag) with ReleaseBoundToTree, ReleaseMixed, ReleaseSurfaceMixed and ReleaseBindingUnobserved, compared against the tree's OBSERVED revision, and ::release_binding_holds refuses Unobserved -- its production consumer is gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_launch_deployment_receipt, which imports it. The release-bin provenance exists too: gunbc.ci.ci_release_bins::ci_emitted_release_bins (dag/gunbc/ci/ci_release_bins.dag), the release-bins.tgz pack, staged and verified by the steps emit.dag names. What is missing is the pairing at the lab: the instance gunbc-srv1-lab-codex-feedback-v0 reads the plan from a detached pinned checkout (operator measurement 2026-11-16: /opt/gunbc-lab/codex-provider-feedback-v0/source at 727573bf) run by an older binary (gunbc 50ef0dd9), so a node merged to main never appears without a hand redeploy, and nothing converges that checkout -- gunbc.live_deploy.release_locus already names the neighbouring hole, that the receipt proves which candidate was copied and not that the bytes match the deploying checkout. The boundary: source and the CI-built binary advance as one pair on a modeled schedule; a main whose release binary is not yet built, or a source/binary disagreement, keeps the last good pair serving and refuses visibly through the binding's own arms -- never a new plan under an old binary, never the reverse, and no hand-maintained lab branch.",
          displaced_cost: "Every merge to main is invisible to the lab until a human redeploys, so the standings the operator reads drift from the plan being worked; and because the pin exists to keep source and binary matched, any ad-hoc advance of one half is a live mismatch risk on the host that dispatches work.",
          first_slice: "Model the pair before touching a unit: one fold deciding, from the observed tree revision and the running release identity, whether the lab's serving pair is bound, and refusing serve when it is not. 3-6 files, 50-150 changed lines, one red-on-base/green-on-head claim -- candidates: a convergence leg over the lab's source root in dag/gunbc/live_deploy/repository_convergence.dag or ::release_locus.dag, the pairing fold beside ::release_binding_holds in dag/gunbc/live_deploy/release_binding.dag, and a claim under dag/test/claim/live_deploy reusing the release-bin provenance of gunbc.ci.ci_release_bins (sha256-verified unpack) rather than building on srv1. Reuse belt_standings_refresh_backstop for the cadence and account for the 13-16 minute fold plus the measured ~19 minute cold restart, since a restart drops serving. If the pair-advance needs a new install or download leg on srv1, that leg is OUT OF SCOPE here and gets its own node: it is a second transition with its own readback, and bundling it would put a unit install behind the same claim as an agreement fold.",
          red_control: "A main head with no matching release binary that gets served anyway, or a stale pair served with no visible refusal: both are green on main today, where no consumer asks the question at the lab at all, and both must refuse on head through the binding's ReleaseMixed or ReleaseBindingUnobserved arm.",
          out_of_scope: "Building binaries on srv1, srv1 unit installs and any new download leg (separate node, separate readback), and the deploy roster that deploy-converge-serve-quiesced and fleet-converge-artifact-closure already own.",
          handback: "The pairing fold with its named consumer on the refresh route, the refusal visible through the existing binding arms, the cadence reused from belt_standings_refresh_backstop with the fold and restart budgets accounted, and the red control executed red-on-base and green-on-head."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "A serving host that cannot converge its own source and binary keeps its prerequisites unfounded: the lab's standings are the readback the roadmap depends on, and today they are converged by hand."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_HU66GO90952PAFHSG2A5UNZQWW",
      id: "lab-converges-release-pair",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/release_locus.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
          headline: "The lab delivers its own release pair: fetch, verify, install and restart as one unit",
          boundary: "One authority transition: the lab's release pair is delivered by a modeled leg instead of by a person standing at a terminal. Measured on main, no host-side delivery route exists. The only consumer of the CI artifact is CI itself: gunbc.fleet.fleet_workflow_steps::ci_release_bins_download_step fetches it and ::ci_release_bins_unpack_verify_step (its run built by gunbc.fleet.fleet_release_bins_key::release_bins_consumer_script_dispatch) verifies the handed-off bins against the build key and runs --verify-build-artifacts fail-closed, unpacking release-bins.tgz (gunbc.ci.ci_release_bins::ci_release_bins_archive_name, rostered by ::ci_emitted_release_bins and ::ci_floor_required_artifact_names) into a job's target/release. Nothing under dag/gunbc/live_deploy fetches an artifact: host units ExecStart from a per-revision release locus that a deploy already installed, addressed by gunbc.live_deploy.release_locus::release_locus_record, ::release_locus_binary_path and ::release_revision_execstart_text over type ReleaseRevisionBinding. gunbc.live_deploy.board_function_converge_unit states the hole in its own commentary -- hosts with no live deployment have no delivery route yet, their plans standing refusing or unrealized until a fleet-wide host-converge release exists. The boundary: a modeled schedule takes one main head, fetches its CI release bins and the matching source, verifies them against the same release provenance, installs BOTH into one new release locus as a single unit, and restarts the lab's serve from it, keeping the last good locus serving whenever any step refuses -- so a node merged to main reaches the served /standings.json with no hand redeploy. The pair it installs must also enter the deploy intent's artifact closure -- gunbc.live_deploy.deploy_artifact_closure::deployment_artifact_read_set, which dag/test/claim/live_deploy/deploy_artifact_closure_witness.dag already executes -- rather than becoming a file on the host that no intent owns. The cadence accounts for the host-side budgets the model already carries (the belt fold measures 13-16 minutes against the unit's 2700-second ceiling, per the comment above gunbc.live_deploy.emit::live_deploy_belt_standings_refresh_unit_file) and for the operator-measured ~19 minute cold restart, since a restart drops serving.",
          displaced_cost: "Every merge waits on a human to move two artifacts that CI already produced and verified, so the lab lags main by however long nobody got to it -- and the hand advance is the same act that can put a new plan under an old binary, which is the failure the pairing node exists to refuse.",
          first_slice: "3-6 files, 50-150 changed lines, one red-on-base/green-on-head claim. Candidates: dag/gunbc/live_deploy/release_locus.dag (one revision binding carrying the source head and the verified release together, so the pair has one identity and the refusal has one place), dag/gunbc/live_deploy/emit.dag (the lab's delivery unit plus its timer, in the timer form gunbc.live_deploy.belt_cadence already emits, calling the existing consumer verify script rather than a new one), and one claim module under dag/test/claim/live_deploy. Reuse gunbc.ci.ci_release_bins for the pack and roster and gunbc.fleet.fleet_workflow_steps plus gunbc.fleet.fleet_release_bins_key for download and verification -- build nothing on srv1, which is why the pairing node declared this leg out of scope rather than shrinking itself. It depends on lab-standings-track-main: installing a pair is only safe once something decides whether a pair is bound.",
          red_control: "A main head whose release bins are not built yet, or whose pack fails the build-key verification, gets installed and served anyway. On base that claim cannot be met at all -- there is no delivery leg to refuse, which is the measurement above -- and on head the same input leaves the last good locus serving with the refusal visible.",
          out_of_scope: "The pairing and binding decision itself (lab-standings-track-main owns it), srv1 provisioning, and a fleet-wide host-converge release for hosts with no live deployment, which gunbc.live_deploy.board_function_converge_unit already names as its own missing authority.",
          handback: "One delivered pair with its readback: the served /standings.json names the source head and the release identity it was built from -- the identity the unit's ExecStart already carries through release_revision_execstart_text -- the timer sits on the modeled cadence with the fold and cold-restart budgets accounted, and any refused step leaves the previous pair serving."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "Convergence that needs a person is not convergence: the lab's release pair has a producer in CI and a consumer on the host, and nothing modeled joins them."),
    ),
    
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_8V8XSA9VQN7C0X0GVW8MSWEZZ1",
      id: "import-less-v2-claim-file-is-executed",
      owner: "self-host",
      carriers: carrier(path: "src/v2/test/claim/provenance/parse_production_extent.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "A v2 claim file with no top-level imports is executed by a claim route, or refused where it is written",
        boundary: "Measured by vivid-swift-693 and eager-bear-290 with a main-built binary on srv1: claim_batch --entry refuses all 9 members of src/v2/test/claim/provenance/parse_production_extent.dag at the checker (unresolved bare std types, RC=1); the native lane (gunbc test //v2/test/claim/provenance/parse_production_extent:all) selects 7 and refuses all 7 at prepare with resolve_reason_unbound_symbol, REFUSED positive_population_empty, RC=2; parse_stamp_span_index shows the same, 0 of 2. The loader's bare-reference edge index binds those names for discovery, but the checker and the native preparer need lexical bindings, so CI parses and typechecks these files and no executor ever runs them. The file was created like this in #13719 (04363f36ef8f, 2026-10-11) and backed an accepted node. Population: 139 of 1129 files under src/v2/test/claim carry no top-level imports; not all are necessarily refused. Related to, and not a duplicate of, gunbc.recurring_failure_mode bare_name_resolved_by_proximity_standing_in_for_resolution, whose standing this row's exit discharges for this population. The design argument is single authority (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority): name binding must not differ between discovery and execution.",
        displaced_cost: "A witness that nothing executes reads as coverage: the roadmap accepts nodes on witnesses that no route has ever run, and the next author of an import-less claim file repeats the shape.",
        first_slice: "The census first: run each of the 139 import-less files on the production claim route, record executed against refused-at-resolve with the reason, and list every ACCEPTED roadmap node whose cited witness is in the refused set. Then the fix, and the row's centering chooses one and states why: make the checker and the native preparer resolve through the same edge index the loader builds, or refuse such a file at parse so it cannot sit in the tree unexecuted.",
        red_control: "After the fix, parse_production_extent and parse_stamp_span_index either execute to a terminal verdict on both claim_batch and the native lane, or are refused at parse with a named reason; on main they are refused at the checker (RC=1) and at prepare (RC=2) and nothing executes them. The exit is the executed whole-corpus resolve that bare_name_resolved_by_proximity_standing_in_for_resolution already demands.",
        out_of_scope: "Adding imports to the individual files as the repair (that hides the divergence between discovery and execution), and widening any budget.",
        handback: "The census table of the 139 files (executed or refused, with reason), the accepted nodes whose witness is in the refused set, the chosen route with its reason, and the before and after runs of the two named files on both routes with their exit statuses."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "compiler-guarantee", contribution: "What a claim file declares is what an executor runs: name binding is one authority across discovery and execution."),
      centering: plan(text: "Target: the claim checker and the native preparer's name binding against the loader's bare-reference edge index; the chosen route is stated here when the census lands."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_XX3GY9X9YR95W5WB5YYC9VMJ0A",
      id: "lab-release-bins-delivery",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/release_pair_delivery.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The lab consumes the CI release-bins pack: verify against the target revision's tree, then extract",
        boundary: "Realizes lab-converges-release-pair's delivery leg; stern-wren-746 has started it ad hoc. The chain is complete without a lab-side --verify-build-artifacts: the manifest digests plus the source_closure check are the proof. Criteria from stern-wren-746: (a) a lab consumer site takes the pack dir plus the TARGET revision's tree and checks kind and source_closure, listing equals roster, manifest equals roster and per-member digests, then extracts; (b) the serve member is derived from gunbc.fleet.fleet_release_bins_key::fleet_release_bins_product().roster ('gunbc'), deleting the free gunbc_serve row; (c) VerifiedOn binds pack_dir plus the target tree.",
        displaced_cost: "The lab installs a pack whose members it did not check against the revision it is serving, or a person moves the artifact by hand; a free gunbc_serve row is a second answer to which member is the served binary.",
        first_slice: "The consumer site of criterion (a), the derivation of the serve member in (b) with the free row deleted, and the VerifiedOn binding of (c), under the same parent as lab-converges-release-pair.",
        red_control: "The discriminator builds a fixture with the real producer's pack statements and adds three reds, each refusing with its own reason: a tampered member, a wrong source_closure and a missing member. Plus an srv1 inhabitance run against the real b691 artifact that refuses at main and admits at head.",
        out_of_scope: "A lab-side --verify-build-artifacts, the pairing decision (lab-standings-track-main), srv1 provisioning, and any building on srv1.",
        handback: "The consumer site and the derivation of the serve member, the three reds each with its refusal reason, and the srv1 inhabitance run's two raw outcomes (main refuses, head admits) with their exit statuses."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "Convergence that needs a person is not convergence: the lab's release pair has a producer in CI and a consumer on the host, and nothing modeled joins them."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_TTZHRWWETX4EQ6HDABTT8AKMCV",
      id: "factory",
      owner: "harness",
      carriers: carrier(path: "docs/plans/harness-work-lifecycle.md"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: level_fields(
          headline: "The factory: harness, workers, CI/floor and the daily workspace, the machine that lands and verifies work",
          boundary: "The steps every change traverses (orientation, implementation, test, commit, publication, review, verification) are owned by the workflow as durable state, not by an agent's memory. CI and floor verdicts name what they executed and cost what their inputs require.",
          approach: "Recursive alignment (harness-recursive-alignment) and prompt projection for the workers; the executed-coverage receipt and whole-population budgets for verification (ci-end-to-end-execution).",
          red_control: "A step the workflow claims to own survives process death and controller restart. A verdict over an empty executed population refuses. A worker crossing two checkpoints on one lineage submits one candidate.",
          displaced_cost: "Every obligation left in prose is re-learned per session and dropped under pressure. Every finished candidate that needs hand-integration spends operator time the factory exists to save.",
          handback: "Per member: the receipt of one journey completed by workflow ownership, and the verification-cost and executed-population figures, named by their instrument."
        ), on: "2026-11-19", note: "Source review at 398136026b345e087968a9db4ba42ec551c5ca4c (2026-11-19); source presence is not acceptance. Project join only; factory children own implementation and verification costs. Its completed-journey handback is a rollup over real receipts, not another uniform repair batch.")),
      parent: in_project(project: "gunbc-project-root", contribution: "The machine that lands and verifies work (harness, workers, CI/floor, daily workspace) is what turns every other project's tasks into landed, verified changes without operator hand-integration."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_F1QXAKK2KYT3GCPMGDZ74V0Y21",
      id: "factory-attempt-execution-on-fabric-lease",
      owner: "harness",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/plans/planning_consolidation.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "Attempt execution holds fabric's lease through the shared attempt lifecycle, and the belt's own slots, placement leases and capture-owed state are deleted",
        boundary: "Execution converges on fabric (gunbc.design_ruling::planning_execution_is_anchored_on_fabric), and gunbc.plans.planning_consolidation records the belt beside it keeping its own attempt state, metering, exit spool, worktree claim and session slots. Three srv1 lab defects (2026-11-04) are that duplicate's symptoms. Bug 10: the drain started a request whose placement lease was already released, because gunbc.roadmap.attempt_system_unit_launch carries the admitted generation only as a nonce; the shared generation check (std.durable_compare_and_set ExpectSlotGeneration, already consumed by gunbc.compute.attempt_lifecycle) answers this. Bug 4: an attempt that predates commit-step records waits forever in CaptureOwed (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_attempt_continuation); capture keyed by the attempt and safe to repeat has no owed state. And gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_belt_actuate runs belt_attempt_slots_cutover_pass on every tick with no gate in code, so a lab redeploy from main migrates its slots without the operator's decision; it ends with the belt's slot store, not with a gate. Measured at da9e9f5ec155 by git grep over dag: CasExpectation in 39 files (2 under gunbc/roadmap), DurableHoldState in 4 (0), file_compare_and_set_windowed in 9 (0). The realization's home awaits the audit planning_consolidation names. This node replaces factory-attempt-slots-cutover-authorized, factory-drain-start-permit-generation-bound and factory-legacy-attempt-capture-owed (#16504), withdrawn by the operator on 2026-11-15 rather than patched on the belt.",
        displaced_cost: "Two attempt and lease systems are kept, every lab defect fixed on the belt is work the cutover deletes, and a lab redeploy from main runs a state migration nobody decided.",
        first_slice: "The audit gunbc.plans.planning_consolidation names: for each belt, attempt, dispatch and session module, what execution needs from it, which fabric or compute authority already answers it (gunbc.compute.attempt_lifecycle, product.capacity.lease, gunbc.fabric.fabric_event_log), and what is deleted; handed to the operator as the realization-home decision before any cut.",
        red_control: "(1) An attempt start whose lease was released, or re-admitted at a newer generation, refuses through the shared generation check and starts nothing. (2) Capture run again on any attempt, including one that predates commit-step records, reaches the same terminal outcome and never rests in an owed state. (3) No module runs belt_attempt_slots_cutover_pass or keeps a roadmap-local slot or lease store. Each RED on main.",
        out_of_scope: "The work-item lifecycle and item events (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_event_log), which stay a separate authority joined to execution by one binding.",
        handback: "The audit and the operator's realization-home decision; then, per cut, the deleted modules, the controls green and the moved citations."
      ), on: "2026-11-19", note: "Source review at 398136026b345e087968a9db4ba42ec551c5ca4c (2026-11-19); source presence is not acceptance. gunbc.fabric.fabric_event_log already supplies generic append/read mechanics, and attempt/lease authorities exist. The residual is deciding execution ownership and moving consumers at actual authority roots. The realization-home decision remains required; eight module names are not eight equivalent cuts.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "factory", contribution: "Work runs on one execution and lease model."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_VE9DT9JA8RC2C17TYAA2ZG2HDZ",
      id: "factory-principal-display-never-raw",
      owner: "harness",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/principal_projection.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "No rendered surface shows a raw principal identifier; a principal that cannot be named renders as a typed error",
        boundary: "Operator direction (2026-11-15): a raw principal identifier on a rendered surface is an error in every case. gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_page event_principal_display renders principal_ref_label as the name of an authenticated principal, and event_principal_display_with_profile falls back to it for any principal with no stored profile, under a stated never-blocks law that renders exactly that fallback. srv1 lab item 12 (the issue page's Return To row) is one instance. principal_ref_label is also the wire label that principal_ref_parse_label reads back, used by about 18 non-test modules at da9e9f5ec155 (git grep -l over dag), so the defect is the wire form used as display text, not the label. Replaces factory-issue-page-principal-display (#16504), which fixed only the issue page.",
        displaced_cost: "The operator reads opaque identifiers on the surfaces they act from and has to look up who a row means, and each surface that adds a principal row can leak one again.",
        first_slice: "Record the operator's direction as a gunbc.design_ruling row superseding the never-blocks fallback, then give the display projection a typed arm for a principal it cannot name, rendered as a visible located error marker instead of the label.",
        red_control: "(1) A page render whose principal has no stored profile contains no raw principal identifier and shows the typed undisplayable marker; RED on main. (2) The render sites a stated search finds using principal_ref_label as display text reach the principal through the one display projection instead, with the search command, revision and inspected population named.",
        out_of_scope: "Profile storage itself, and wire and storage uses of principal_ref_label.",
        handback: "The ruling row, the projection change, the controls with the red run on main, and the moved render sites with the search stated."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "factory", contribution: "The daily workspace is readable without lookups."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_XR5XF8MTD80RQ4YGTV7Q6ZNK08",
      id: "deploy-converge-serve-quiesced",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/repository_convergence.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "Repository convergence on a serving host succeeds without anyone stopping serve by hand",
        boundary: "srv1 lab bug 14 (2026-11-04), still open: gunbc.live_deploy.repository_convergence refuses with changed binding during the transition whenever a linked worktree changes mid-converge, and serve creates and removes snapshot worktrees continuously, so every converge on a serving host needs serve and the agent door stopped by hand (the T6 cutover runbook, #16461, still does this with unit-identity fences). Either the convergence excludes serve's transient snapshot worktrees by a declared rule, or quiescing serve is a modeled step of the transition with its own readback. gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_report says the quiescence ordering is unsettled and cites gunbc.plans.deploy_convergence_observed_side, a plan #16107 deleted; settle the ordering here and move those citations.",
        displaced_cost: "Every deploy to the lab or production costs an operator or agent a hand stop and start of serve, which is exactly the out-of-band actuation the deploy model exists to remove, and a missed restart leaves the service down.",
        first_slice: "Decide exclude versus quiesce with the reason stated, then implement the chosen arm for serve's snapshot worktrees.",
        red_control: "(1) A convergence run while a fixture serve creates and removes a snapshot worktree completes; RED on main, where it refuses. (2) A change to a non-transient linked worktree during the transition still refuses.",
        out_of_scope: "The deploy's artifact closure (deploy-intent-owns-artifact-closure).",
        handback: "The decision and its reason, the change, the two controls with the red run on main, and the moved citations."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "The live deploy path converges a serving host without a hand step."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_883KZ50W4VJVKZYKKP7WAEWVPZ",
      id: "fleet-leg-wall-clock-deadline",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/fleet/fleet_known_hosts_anchor.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "Every fleet transport leg carries a declared, per-kind runtime wall-clock deadline and refuses by name as a value instead of waiting forever",
        boundary: "A fleet ssh leg is a subprocess the runtime host-effect sink spawns, and nothing bounds its total wall time. the fleet_ssh_leg_deadline data row of gunbc.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor says EVERY FLEET LEG CARRIES A DEADLINE, and that one authority carries connect=10s, alive_interval=15s, alive_count_max=4 -- but those become only ConnectTimeout (bounds the TCP connect) and ServerAliveInterval x ServerAliveCountMax (bounds an established path that goes dark, per-master), emitted by gunbc.fleet.fleet_known_hosts_anchor::fleet_ssh_leg_deadline_options. The comment beside that data row already admits the gap: the sshd answers keepalives itself, so a remote command that is merely silent is NOT cut off. fleet-converge run 44488679086, job desired-instance-deploy, step 'Deploy the named srv1 instance (live_deploy_apply_desired_wet_from_ci)' proves it: the leg git -C /opt/gunbc/gunbc rev-parse HEAD on srv1 (run_typed_argv_transport of FleetSsh from gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_report::observe_deployed_tree_at into gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_observation::deployed_tree_leg) started 11:59:25 and never returned; the only backstop was the 30-minute GitHub step timeout and no receipt was written. The same leg outside CI, same options, ControlMaster and BatchMode, returns in 0.26s, so the hang is in the runner environment or the sink, not git or the ssh options. The bound must be added where the subprocess is spawned: the transport-shell sink dispatch_shell in src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs already routes every fleet leg (the ssh transport of extdeps/ssh/session.dag AND gunbc.WitnessBin.Run of extdeps/gunbc.dag, both declared transport shell) through wait_child_honoring_wall_deadline, but that honors only the whole-receipt ctx.witness_wall_deadline -- when that is unset it falls through to an unbounded capture_child_output / child.wait in bounded_shell_host_drain.rs, and when armed its refusal is the generic EvaluationBudgetExceeded naming the entry, never the host, argv and deadline of the leg that stalled. The model has gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_observation::TreeLegNotAttempted (a leg that never ran) but no arm for a leg that started and exceeded a wall-clock deadline. The deadline VALUE is authored at ONE authority as a per-kind table: each FleetRemoteLeg construction site declares a typed FleetLegKind = ProbeLeg | ActuationLeg | RepositoryConvergenceLeg | ProvisioningLeg carried on the leg (never inferred from argv or command text), and fleet_ssh_leg_deadline carries one wall per kind -- ProbeLeg 15s, ActuationLeg 60s, RepositoryConvergenceLeg 300s, ProvisioningLeg 900s. The transport-shell request gains an optional wall deadline that the caller fills from the leg's kind, and its output a typed outcome completed(exit/stdout/stderr) | overran\{deadline\}; the overrun is a VALUE, not an InterpError, so the .dag layer can catch it and reach the receipt (an error would abort evaluation and be uncatchable). The sink honors exactly the deadline the caller passes -- it never classifies argv[0] -- so gunbc.WitnessBin.Run and any other non-fleet dispatch simply passes no deadline and keeps today's behavior under the outer whole-receipt budget; only fleet legs carry one, so the table has no 'none' row. ConnectTimeout and ServerAlive stay exactly as authored, because they bound different things (connect and dark-path); no ssh keyword bounds a live connection whose remote command never returns, which is why the total-wall bound must be enforced by the runtime that owns the child process, not by ssh options.",
        displaced_cost: "One hung leg parks the whole fleet-converge or deploy job until the 30-minute GitHub step timeout kills it, writes no receipt, and leaves the run with nothing to read afterwards -- so a real finding reads as an infrastructure flake. And the whole-receipt wall budget, where armed, can only refuse with a budget-exceeded naming the entry, so no operator learns which host and which command overran.",
        first_slice: "Declare a typed FleetLegKind on the FleetRemoteLeg construction sites, add a per-kind wall table to FleetSshLegDeadline authored in the fleet_ssh_leg_deadline data row (ProbeLeg 15s, ActuationLeg 60s, RepositoryConvergenceLeg 300s, ProvisioningLeg 900s), give the transport-shell request an optional wall deadline plus a typed output outcome completed | overran\{deadline\}, and have dispatch_shell honor exactly the caller-passed deadline (always in the child's own process group, killing it on trip) and return overran as a value. run_typed_argv_transport then maps overran to a located TreeLegDeadlineExceeded\{host, argv, deadline\}, the located naming coming from the leg which already holds host and argv.",
        red_control: "(1) A Rust control: a leg whose argv sleeps past the deadline (argv 'sleep' against a 2s per-leg deadline) returns overran and is refused by name within the bound and within test wall -- proves the deadline is enforced as a value, not an abort. (2) A green control: a leg that finishes inside the deadline still converges with its exit code and output, so the deadline is not merely always firing. (3) A .dag claim: the incident leg itself -- deployed_tree_leg's git rev-parse HEAD on srv1, a ProbeLeg -- overrunning at its 15s deadline maps to DeployedTreeUnobservable{ TreeLegDeadlineExceeded\{host, argv, deadline\} } AND a receipt is still written on that arm; this is the incident's real failure, the missing receipt.",
        out_of_scope: "Retrying a refused leg (an overrun is a terminal typed value, never a retry trigger); the ConnectTimeout and ServerAlive values themselves (per-master connection and keepalive bounds, unchanged here); the whole-receipt evaluation wall budget, which stays a separate outer bound that this per-leg ceiling sits under; any per-leg deadline on non-fleet dispatches such as gunbc.WitnessBin.Run's long witness runs (they pass none); and any 'none' row in the table (the table covers fleet legs only).",
        handback: "The FleetLegKind type and EVERY FleetRemoteLeg construction site named in the PR body with the kind it was given (the incident's deployed_tree_leg git rev-parse is a ProbeLeg); the per-kind wall table and the reason for each number; the transport-shell request deadline field and completed | overran\{deadline\} output; the sink enforcement in dispatch_shell honoring only the caller-passed deadline and returning overran as a value; the new DeployedTreeUnobservableCause arm TreeLegDeadlineExceeded in deployed_tree_observation.dag and the deployed_tree_report.dag receipt line that carries host, argv and deadline so the run always leaves a receipt; and the three controls (two Rust, one .dag receipt claim) with their red and green runs."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "A serving host converged over ssh whose leg can hang forever has no bounded, located refusal; every fleet leg carries a declared, per-kind deadline the runtime enforces."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_Z0G9G30XDNQJG7QYJ0M6NAVY0T",
      id: "deploy-intent-owns-artifact-closure",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/intent.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "A deploy installs every artifact its units need from one intent, and its install receipt reproduces",
        boundary: "srv1 lab bugs 5 and 6 (2026-11-04). Bug 5, a hand deploy that did not install the drain's helper snapshot (every start exited 127 for about five hours), is mitigated: #15826 added gunbc.live_deploy.intent DeploymentIntent { instance_id, revision }, whose only consumer gunbc.live_deploy.apply_legs attempt_helper_snapshot_closure verifies the snapshot before the drain is enabled, and #15905 made the receipt check kinds, links, modes and ownership. Still open: the intent does not own the full artifact closure (which units need which installed artifacts), so the next missing artifact is found the same way. Bug 6, the helper-locus receipt digest not reproducing, has no recorded cause; gunbc.host.host_effect_realize now sorts receipt lines bytewise, which may or may not be the fix.",
        displaced_cost: "Each artifact a unit needs and the deploy does not own is another outage found from the logs of a failing service.",
        first_slice: "Establish whether bug 6 still reproduces: install the same tree twice and compare the receipt digests. Then enumerate the artifacts each deployed unit reads and name which the intent already owns.",
        red_control: "(1) Two installs of the same revision produce the same receipt digest. (2) A deploy whose intent omits an artifact a unit reads refuses before any unit is enabled; RED for each artifact the closure does not yet own.",
        out_of_scope: "Serve quiescence during converge (deploy-converge-serve-quiesced).",
        handback: "The bug 6 reading with its command and revision, the artifact inventory, and the closure change with its controls."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "A deploy cannot leave a unit without what it needs."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_1EZWCJ1ZXZ00JK5C9E7EJT7VQF",
      id: "deploy-apply-deadline-covers-cold-start",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/service_ready.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The dashboard-deploy step's deadline is derived from its own terms, its readiness wait can observe a cold compile, and an interrupted fold is recorded by a surviving observer or reconciled independently -- never guessed at from an absent receipt",
        boundary: "Fleet-converge run 45322329169, job 138208015742 (dashboard-deploy; plan revision 44808cbe, 2026-11-24): step 15 (fleet_converge_dashboard_deploy_step at fleet_converge_workflow.dag:5234, the live-deploy apply fold) was CANCELLED at 06:10:27Z after 45.5 min; step 16 (fleet_converge_dashboard_receipt_assert_step at fleet_converge_workflow.dag:2292) FAILED, and the receipt upload step failed with it. The job's post steps (Post WIF auth, Post Checkout, Complete job) completed, so the runner SURVIVED this cancel, and step 16 ran because its if_condition is always() and it is fed the deploy step's conclusion (GUNBC_DEPLOY_STEP_CONCLUSION = steps.dashboard_deploy.conclusion, apply_receipt.dag:789). The cancel CAUSE is unattributed from the job record: not the step timeout (timeout-minutes: 60 at .github/workflows/fleet-converge.yml:3718, from gunbc_ci_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_minutes: Duration = 60 at fleet_workflow_steps.dag:929, against a 45.5 min step) and not the job backstop (344 min, derived at fleet_workflow_steps.dag:1127, against a 2h07m job); the concurrency group gunbc-host-mutation-srv1 has cancel-in-progress: false and nothing newer ran in it, and the org audit log returned 404. What the record does show: the readiness poll probed 127.0.0.1:8080/healthz 136 times (06:02:43-06:10:27Z), all exit=7; the serve first answered at 06:17:40Z reporting 44808cbe -- about 15 min (~900 s) after units restarted at ~06:02:43Z, beyond the poll bound of 720 s. So the target subsequently served revision 44808cbe while the cancelled attempt's own outcome remained UNKNOWN: an absent receipt establishes neither success nor failure. THE ROW DOES NOT ASSUME A LONGER TIMEOUT FIXES THIS RUN. TWO INDEPENDENT DEFECTS UNDERNEATH. FIRST: live_deploy_roadmap_unit_expected_startup: Second = second(count: 240) at service_ready.dag:75 was calibrated 2026-09-04 against a 219 s cold source-load with 611 sources; the derived poll bound (240*3 = 720 s at service_ready.dag:139) sits below the ~900 s cold compile measured at 44808cbe, and the comment at service_ready.dag:70 already acknowledges the cold start is not a steady cost. SECOND: gunbc_ci_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_minutes at fleet_workflow_steps.dag:929 is a hand-set constant (raised 30 to 60 under operator ruling srv1_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_raised at design_ruling.dag:372), not composed from the apply-legs bound plus the poll bound, so the two silently decouple when the cold-restart calibration changes. ONE FALSE CLAIM IN THE CURRENT MODEL, MEASURED HERE: apply_receipt.dag:803 refuses an absent receipt on the ground that 'the transaction mints a document on every terminal arm (apply or named refusal)' -- but a fold cancelled from outside never reaches an arm, so an absent document carries NO information about the target's real state. Operator ruling ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth (design_ruling.dag) closes CI time budgets to growth without explicit sign-off: the derivation is the requirement, and any derived value over 60 minutes goes to the operator with its measured parts; that is not a pre-authorised budget increase.",
        displaced_cost: "Every cold deploy (one whose closure was not previously compiled on srv1) can outrun the readiness poll before the serve binds: the fold can return ServiceNotReady for a deploy that succeeds. And an interrupted fold leaves no document at all. When the runner survives, the job's always()-fed observer records the absence, but nothing names what the target actually did; when the runner is lost, no step of that job runs at all, and the absent receipt must not be read as an apply failure -- run 45322329169 shows an absent receipt beside a serve that turned healthy afterwards. A successful deploy is left indistinguishable from a failed one.",
        first_slice: "(a) Attribute the cancel of run 45322329169 step 15 from the run and job API records (a runner loss, a newer arrival in the concurrency group, a manual cancel, or the step's own timeout); UNKNOWN until read, never inferred. (b) Derive gunbc_ci_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_minutes from its named terms (the composition pattern of srv1_root_mutation_approval_step_timeout_minutes at srv1_root_mutation_approval_run.dag:157), with the readiness poll bound as an explicit component, and regenerate fleet-converge.yml from the authority -- never by hand. If the derived value exceeds 60 minutes, present it with its measured parts to the operator before landing (ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth). (c) Say what a SURVIVING observer can report, and report only that: the job already runs fleet_converge_dashboard_receipt_assert_step under always() fed the deploy step's conclusion (fleet_converge_workflow.dag:2292), and its zero-document verdict AssertZeroReceipts (apply_receipt.dag:847) already says the fold died before the transaction ran. That verdict must stay unknown-admitting: an absent document is never rewritten into an apply outcome. Correct the claim at apply_receipt.dag:803 that every terminal arm mints a document -- an externally cancelled fold reaches no arm. (d) Name the lost-runner case and hand it to its own frontier rather than carrying it open-ended: when the runner is lost no step of the deploy job runs, so there is no inside observer to strengthen. The read belongs to deploy-reconcile-lost-runner-receipt, whose trigger is a dashboard-deploy run holding zero receipt documents while the deploy step concluded cancelled or failed, or a run whose job record shows no post steps at all. (e) Name the ~900 s cold compile before healthz as its own open question -- the DISSOLUTION TRIGGER note above live_deploy_service_ready_poll_bound at service_ready.dag:139 already sizes that bound around this cost-shape defect -- with the two alternatives (a prebuilt release pack, or a background start gated on readiness), so the poll bound is not the only lever: a widened wait pays the full window on every hung deploy, and the deadline is the floor, not the fix.",
        red_control: "(1) The readiness poll bound is at least the measured cold-compile bound at the calibration revision; RED at main, where the bound is 720 s against a ~900 s cold compile. (2) gunbc_ci_dashboard_deploy_step_timeout_minutes is composed from named terms rather than a hand-set Duration constant; RED at main, where it is a bare integer. (3) Witness on dashboard_receipt_assert_verdict_text (apply_receipt.dag:799) at the constructed input of zero documents with the deploy step concluded 'cancelled': its text names an externally terminated fold and outcome UNKNOWN, not a document minted on every terminal arm; RED at main, where the text makes the unbounded claim. (4) Constructed pair: (i) zero documents with the deploy step concluded 'cancelled' (the surviving-observer case, run 45322329169) and (ii) a job record with no post steps at all (the lost-runner signature). The model must claim different things about them -- (i) a surviving observer reports the absence and hands the target's state to a named frontier, (ii) nothing inside the job claims anything; RED at main, where (i) is reported only as a failed assertion whose text asserts the minting claim that (ii) shows false.",
        out_of_scope: "Eliminating the cold compile itself (demand-serve-program-image, demand-serve-entry-compile-time), per-ssh-leg transport wall-clock bounds (fleet-leg-wall-clock-deadline), the reconciliation read for a lost runner (deploy-reconcile-lost-runner-receipt, whose trigger this row names), and the cause of run 45322329169's cancel beyond what its run and job records show.",
        handback: "The cancel attribution for run 45322329169, the composed step-timeout with its measured parts (and the operator's decision if it exceeds 60 min), the corrected zero-document claim in apply_receipt.dag with its witness, the named frontier for the lost-runner read with its trigger stated, the regenerated fleet-converge.yml matching its generator, and the four controls green."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "A deploy that succeeds leaves a receipt; a deploy whose budget cannot observe a cold start leaves no receipt and cannot be told from one that failed."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_5C12MKCE5T847QAS2K048KS06N",
      id: "deploy-reconcile-lost-runner-receipt",
      owner: "debt",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/apply_receipt.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "A live-deploy run that leaves no receipt document at all is reconciled by an observer outside the deploy job, which records what the target reports and names no cause",
        boundary: "THE FRONTIER OPENED BY deploy-apply-deadline-covers-cold-start, named here so the hand-off has a home with a trigger instead of an open-ended carry. The dashboard-deploy job's own receipt assertion (fleet_converge_dashboard_receipt_assert_step at fleet_converge_workflow.dag:2292) carries if_condition always() and is fed the deploy step conclusion (live_deploy_deploy_conclusion_env_name, apply_receipt.dag:789), so it CAN report an absent document when the runner survives -- measured in run 45322329169 job 138208015742: step 15 cancelled at 06:10:27Z, step 16 FAILED as an assertion, the receipt upload failed (if-no-files-found: error), and the job's post steps completed. When the runner itself is lost, no step of that job runs at all: no document and no verdict exist inside it, and an absent document carries NO information about the target. What the same run establishes about the target, and nothing more: srv1 answered healthz at 06:17:40Z, 7 minutes AFTER the job was cancelled, reporting revision 44808cbe -- so the target subsequently served that revision, while the interrupted attempt's own outcome remained UNKNOWN. A durable record for the lost-runner case therefore needs an authority OUTSIDE the deploy job; no post-step inside the job can supply one, and treating the absence as an apply failure would fabricate an outcome. No existing row owns this read: deploy-apply-deadline-covers-cold-start bounds the deadline and the surviving-observer claim, and fleet-leg-wall-clock-deadline bounds the transport legs.",
        displaced_cost: "A lost runner leaves a deploy unrecorded: the run shows a cancelled step and no artifact, the next dispatch cannot say whether the target holds the previous revision or the new one, and any rollback or re-dispatch is decided with no observation of the target.",
        first_slice: "(a) The trigger, stated as a readable condition over run records: a dashboard-deploy run whose receipt directory holds zero documents while the deploy step conclusion is cancelled or failed, or whose job record shows no post steps at all -- the signature a lost runner leaves. (b) An observer OUTSIDE the deploy job reads the target's serve state at the modeled readiness bound (live_deploy_service_ready_poll_bound at service_ready.dag:139) and records an observation: the revision the serve reports, whether healthz answered, and the poll count -- with no cause field, because this observer did not see a cause. (c) The fixture for the trigger keeps the receipt directory EMPTY, and that is the point: admit_dashboard_deploy_receipts (apply_receipt.dag:841) reaches AssertZeroReceipts (:847), which stays the unknown-admitting verdict rather than an apply failure, and the observation is recorded OUTSIDE that directory so the assertion never reads it. AssertMultipleReceipts (:849) fires only when two JSON documents sit in that directory, which this row never creates; where a genuine apply or refusal receipt IS present, the run is not the lost-runner case at all.",
        red_control: "(1) Constructed input: an EMPTY receipt directory, the deploy step concluded 'cancelled', and a target serve reporting the deployed revision -- the receipt assertion still refuses as AssertZeroReceipts (:847), unknown-admitting and not an apply failure, and a separate observation record exists naming that revision and the poll count; RED at main in its second half, where nothing outside the job records anything and the run's only trace is a failed assertion. (2) The observation type declares no cause field, so a constructed attempt to attribute a cause refuses at the record site; RED at main, where no observation type exists to refuse it. (3) Misplacement control: a mutant that drops the observation document INTO the receipt directory refuses as AssertUnreadableReceipt (classify_dashboard_receipt_document at apply_receipt.dag:817 tries the apply schema then the refusal schema and names both reasons; mapped to the verdict at :864) -- NOT AssertMultipleReceipts, which requires two documents in that directory (:849); RED at main, where no such kind exists to be misplaced.",
        out_of_scope: "Bounding the step deadline and the readiness poll (deploy-apply-deadline-covers-cold-start), eliminating the cold compile (demand-serve-program-image, demand-serve-entry-compile-time), and deciding what to dispatch once the observation is in hand.",
        handback: "The trigger read over a real run record, the observation kind with an executed read of the target serve state at the modeled bound, the empty-directory case still refusing as AssertZeroReceipts, the misplaced-observation mutant refusing as AssertUnreadableReceipt by name, and the three controls green."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "convergence-coverage", contribution: "An unrecorded deploy outcome is reconciled by an outside observation, never inferred from the absence of a document."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_MGB7PA8AHA6B26CRK6R36G6HZS",
      id: "demand-serve-program-image",
      owner: "self-host",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/plans/demand_engine_program.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The roadmap serve entry runs as a native-served program, so a restart starts the stored built product instead of resolving and typing its closure again",
        boundary: "srv1 lab items 9 and 13 (2026-11-04): restarting serve takes about 11 minutes, and each harness worker rests at about 6.7 GiB. Measured on fleet-converge run 43637222454 (serve_entry_verdict, 1436-module closure): resolve_entry_graph_inclusive is about 175 of 176 s of the pre-entry, peak RSS about 5.7 GiB. Serve runs on gunbc run, the v1 seed interpreter, so persisting a program image inside it is SeedFeatureCompletion and refused (gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing::v1_seed_standing). The admitted route is native serving: the entry runs as a native serve driver (gunbc.native_serve), resolve and typing run once at build time, and the built product is already stored, keyed and re-verified by the seed's native product cache, which refuses a mismatched or corrupted product and rebuilds. Folding that cache's key into std.materialization_provider request_key is owed only when its own stated trigger holds (the provider serves the native artifact as its only producer). The seed-embedded descriptor gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_native_ingress.roadmap_native_ingress_program carries this node's realized state: startup is Absent -- the startup handler is deferred and owed on this node.",
        displaced_cost: "Every deploy holds the service down for the restart, and every worker's resting memory limits how many workers a host can run.",
        first_slice: "Run the roadmap serve entry natively off srv1 and name each refusal between it and serving; fix those at their earliest unjustified boundary.",
        red_control: "(1) The natively served roadmap entry answers a fixed request set with the same responses as the interpreted serve at the same revision; RED on main, where it does not serve natively. (2) A restart with an unchanged closure and compiler starts the stored product without resolve or typing, with the pre-entry receipt showing it. (3) A changed closure or compiler digest, or a corrupted stored product, rebuilds and never serves the stale product.",
        out_of_scope: "Reducing the cold compile itself (demand-serve-entry-compile-time), and moving srv1 lab or production units onto the native route, which is a deploy step its owner takes.",
        handback: "The refusals found and fixed, the three controls, and restart time and resting memory before and after, measured off srv1 at one revision."
      )),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "demand-engine", contribution: "The served program is materialized once per key and reused across restarts."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_7TVTYN2VYB4NSQH9YHZ2Q654R3",
      id: "demand-serve-entry-compile-time",
      owner: "self-host",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_ingress.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: updated(t: fields(
        headline: "Compiling the roadmap serve entry fits back under the 30-minute verdict step",
        boundary: "On srv1 at 2026-11-14, gunbc compile --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_ingress.dag took 1607 s at 13.6 GB peak, so #16485 raised the fleet-converge serve_entry_verdict step from 30 to 45 minutes with the operator's sign-off (gunbc.design_ruling::serve_entry_verdict_step_raised_to_45_minutes), naming runtime reduction as the selected remediation. Nothing owns that reduction. Find where the compile spends its time (closure size against resolve and typing cost per module) and remove the cost at its earliest unjustified boundary (gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary); then lower the step bound, which needs no sign-off.",
        displaced_cost: "Every production deploy waits about 27 minutes on one compile, and the job's time budget grew to absorb it.",
        first_slice: "A phase breakdown of the 1607 s compile on one revision, with the closure's module count.",
        red_control: "The budget governs the program the step runs, not the compile form: gunbc run with entry spec.service.serve_entry and the http ingress binding arguments (record_plan_serve_entry_verdict_for via observe_served_command_reaches_bind). At a named revision that program completes under 30 minutes on srv1 with its receipt, and only then the step bound returns to 30. A whole-closure gunbc compile of the entry is a different program, measured separately below and not governed by this budget.",
        out_of_scope: "Persisting the program across restarts (demand-serve-program-image).",
        handback: "The phase breakdown, the change, the measured compile time before and after at the same revision, and the lowered bound."
      ), on: "2026-11-23", note: "Premise restated (warm-fox-517 from gentle-tern-663, read at 957fa04f with gunbc 83c2770d): the step no longer runs the compile form. record_plan_serve_entry_compile_verdict reaches observe_served_command_reaches_bind, which runs gunbc run, not observe_serve_entry_compile_clean (the compile-to-dag argv), and the in-file comment records that the step was changed from the whole-closure compile for that reason. Measured: the compile form the boundary names is 1871.79 s wall, user 1858.81, max RSS 14,794,308 kB, exit 0, closure about 3666 files, 1,798,764 of 1,871,790 ms unattributed inside compile_transaction; the step command is 943.26 s wall, user 930.85, max RSS 8,639,588 kB, closure_files 1454, resolve_entry_graph_inclusive 212,718 ms, exit 1 only at the final write to /tmp/fleet-converge-plan/serve_entry_verdict.json (Permission denied, os error 13). So the 45-minute bound guards a program the step no longer runs. The 27-minute displaced cost in this row belongs to the whole-closure compile and no longer to the deploy step; owner decision (warm-fox-517): the runtime reduction is kept, but not under this row's deploy framing; it is filed as its own row, serve-entry-compile-inhabitance-wet-lane-cost.")),
      dispatch: dispatchable(),
      parent: in_project(project: "demand-engine", contribution: "A deploy pays for the serve entry's compile only what the change requires."),
    ),
    roadmap_node(
      identity: "rn_3J8ET2JKC02CWJT8WMH4Y6F953",
      id: "fleet-cap-bracket-keeps-running-units",
      owner: "fleet",
      carriers: carrier(path: "dag/gunbc/runner/runner_host_cap_bracket.dag"),
      line: ticket(t: fields(
        headline: "The runner cap bracket never masks a running unit",
        boundary: "Runner srv4-08 could not read its own cgroup memory.events (CgroupFileUnreadable), failing v2-bootstrap on #15830 (2026-11-04); the cause is gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_running_unit_masked_loses_its_drop_ins_at_the_next_reload (srv4-01, 02, 08, 12 and 15). #15838 fixed the slot-tree path; the same record says gunbc.runner.runner_host_cap_bracket still masks running units. Draft #16035 (proud-heron-92) touches the cap bracket; reconcile with it before starting so this node does not fork that work.",
        displaced_cost: "A masked running runner fails required CI jobs at random, and each failure costs a rerun and someone's time reading the log.",
        first_slice: "Read #16035 and the recurring failure record; either adopt #16035's change as this node's realization or make the bracket skip active units.",
